</span></span></span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">The packing stability in symplectic geometry was first noticed by Biran <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bir97</a>]</cite>: the symplectic obstructions to embed several balls into a manifold disappear when their size is small enough. This phenomenon is known to hold for all closed manifolds with rational symplectic class (see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bir99</a>]</cite> for the <math id="abstract1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional case, and <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx1" title="" class="ltx_ref">BH11</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx2" title="" class="ltx_ref">BH13</a>]</cite> for higher dimensions), as well as for all ellipsoids <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx2" title="" class="ltx_ref">BH13</a>]</cite>.</p>
    
<p id="abstract1.2" class="ltx_p">In this note, we show that packing stability holds for all closed, and several open, symplectic <math id="abstract1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math>-manifolds.</p>
  
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bir97</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bir99</a>]</cite>, Biran discovered the packing stability phenomenon: in some symplectic <math id="S1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math>-manifolds, the symplectic obstructions to pack <math id="S1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> identical balls disappear when <math id="S1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> becomes large enough. He later generalized this result to every <span id="S1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">closed</span> symplectic <math id="S1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math>-manifold with rational symplectic class (<math id="S1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\omega]\in H^{2}(M,\mathbb{Q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\omega]\in H^{2}(M,\mathbb{Q})</annotation></semantics></math>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In this paper we generalize these results in several directions, by applying the singular inflation technique developed in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ops13b</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx9" title="" class="ltx_ref">MO13</a>]</cite>. We generalize Biran’s packing stability to all symplectic <math id="S1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math>-manifolds, and also to a class of open symplectic <math id="S1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math>-manifolds including ellipsoids and domains we call pseudo-balls.
For these manifolds, we establish not just packing stability but strong packing stability, which we define now. The definition is in the spirit of Biran’s first results on the subject <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bir97</a>]</cite>. Throughout <math id="S1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B(\lambda)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B(\lambda)</annotation></semantics></math> will denote an open ball of capacity <math id="S1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> (see Definition <a href="#S1.Thmdefinition3" title="Definition 1.3. ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Packing stability for symplectic 4-manifolds." class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.3</span></a>) and we will write <math id="S1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U\overset{\omega}{\hookrightarrow}V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">𝜔</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U\overset{\omega}{\hookrightarrow}V</annotation></semantics></math> for a symplectic embedding.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmdefinition1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmdefinition1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 1.1</span></span><span id="S1.Thmdefinition1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmdefinition1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmdefinition1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.Thmdefinition1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">A symplectic manifold <math id="S1.Thmdefinition1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> has property <math id="S1.Thmdefinition1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}(\lambda)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}(\lambda)</annotation></semantics></math> if for all collection of real numbers <math id="S1.Thmdefinition1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\lambda_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\lambda_{i})</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S1.Thmdefinition1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{i}&lt;\lambda\;\forall i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0.447em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{i}&lt;\lambda\;\forall i</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S1.Thmdefinition1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sqcup B(\lambda_{i})\overset{\omega}{\hookrightarrow}X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0em">⊔</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">𝜔</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sqcup B(\lambda_{i})\overset{\omega}{\hookrightarrow}X</annotation></semantics></math> if and only if the volume obstruction <math id="S1.Thmdefinition1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{1}{2}\sum\lambda_{i}^{2}\leq\mathrm{Vol}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>∑</mo><msubsup><mi>λ</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>Vol</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{1}{2}\sum\lambda_{i}^{2}\leq\mathrm{Vol}(X)</annotation></semantics></math> is satisfied. We say that <math id="S1.Thmdefinition1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> has the strong packing stability if it satisfies <math id="S1.Thmdefinition1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}(\lambda)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}(\lambda)</annotation></semantics></math> for some <math id="S1.Thmdefinition1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math>. We then define</span></p>
<table id="S1.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda(X):=\inf\{\lambda\;|\;X\text{ verifies }\mathcal{P}(\lambda)\}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">:=</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0.1389em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">inf</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo>|</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic"> verifies </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda(X):=\inf\{\lambda\;|\;X\text{ verifies }\mathcal{P}(\lambda)\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmdefinition1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmdefinition1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.Thmdefinition1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">If there exists a <math id="S1.Thmdefinition1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math> such that for all <math id="S1.Thmdefinition1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda&lt;\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>μ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda&lt;\mu</annotation></semantics></math> we have <math id="S1.Thmdefinition1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sqcup B(\lambda)\overset{\omega}{\hookrightarrow}X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0em">⊔</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">𝜔</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sqcup B(\lambda)\overset{\omega}{\hookrightarrow}X</annotation></semantics></math> if and only if the volume obstruction is satisfied, then we say that <math id="S1.Thmdefinition1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> has packing stability. (In other words, we only consider embeddings by equally sized balls.)</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmdefinition2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_rk">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmdefinition2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 1.2</span></span><span id="S1.Thmdefinition2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmdefinition2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmdefinition2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.Thmdefinition2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Since the volume inequality used is not strict, this definition even of packing stability is stronger than those used previously (for example in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx1" title="" class="ltx_ref">BH11</a>]</cite>) which ask for embeddings only if the union of balls has strictly smaller volume. Of course, to obtain embeddings in the case of equal volume, it is necessary for us to work with open balls. Such embeddings were called very full fillings in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx5" title="" class="ltx_ref">LMS12</a>]</cite>, section <math id="S1.Thmdefinition2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="7" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>7</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">7</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Our main result is the following.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmthm1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_thm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmthm1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 1</span></span><span id="Thmthm1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmthm1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmthm1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmthm1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">All closed <math id="Thmthm1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional symplectic manifolds have strong packing stability.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">To prove this theorem we will first need to establish strong packing stability for some open symplectic manifolds, and these results are interesting in themselves. To define these domains we work in <math id="S1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{C}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{C}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> with its standard symplectic form.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmdefinition3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmdefinition3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 1.3</span></span><span id="S1.Thmdefinition3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmdefinition3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmdefinition3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.Thmdefinition3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S1.Thmdefinition3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U\subset\mathbb{C}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U\subset\mathbb{C}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> be the interior of a domain of the form <math id="S1.Thmdefinition3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{(z,w)\in\mathbb{C}^{2}\;|\;(\pi|z|^{2},\pi|w|^{2})\in P\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{(z,w)\in\mathbb{C}^{2}\;|\;(\pi|z|^{2},\pi|w|^{2})\in P\}</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S1.Thmdefinition3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> is a subset of the first quadrant in <math id="S1.Thmdefinition3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmdefinition3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmdefinition3.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.Thmdefinition3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">If <math id="S1.Thmdefinition3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> is the convex hull <math id="S1.Thmdefinition3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P=\text{Conv}\,\langle(0,0),(a,0),(0,b)\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">Conv</mtext><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P=\text{Conv}\,\langle(0,0),(a,0),(0,b)\rangle</annotation></semantics></math> then we say <math id="S1.Thmdefinition3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math> is a symplectic ellipsoid <math id="S1.Thmdefinition3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E(a,b)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E(a,b)</annotation></semantics></math>. A ball of capacity <math id="S1.Thmdefinition3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math> is an ellipsoid <math id="S1.Thmdefinition3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B(a)=E(a,a)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B(a)=E(a,a)</annotation></semantics></math>, and we write <math id="S1.Thmdefinition3.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau E(a,b)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau E(a,b)</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S1.Thmdefinition3.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E(\tau a,\tau b)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E(\tau a,\tau b)</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmdefinition3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmdefinition3.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.Thmdefinition3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">If <math id="S1.Thmdefinition3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P=\text{Conv}\,\langle(0,0),(0,a),(b,0),(\alpha,\beta)\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">Conv</mtext><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>β</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P=\text{Conv}\,\langle(0,0),(0,a),(b,0),(\alpha,\beta)\rangle</annotation></semantics></math> for some <math id="S1.Thmdefinition3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a&gt;\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a&gt;\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S1.Thmdefinition3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b&gt;\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b&gt;\beta</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.Thmdefinition3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a,b&lt;\alpha+\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a,b&lt;\alpha+\beta</annotation></semantics></math> then we say that <math id="S1.Thmdefinition3.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math> is a pseudo-ball <math id="S1.Thmdefinition3.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T(a,b,\alpha,\beta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>β</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T(a,b,\alpha,\beta)</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Now we can state the following.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmthm2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_thm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmthm2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 2</span></span><span id="Thmthm2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmthm2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmthm2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmthm2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The <math id="Thmthm2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional ellipsoids have the strong packing stability. Moreover, the function <math id="Thmthm2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda(E(1,a))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda(E(1,a))</annotation></semantics></math> is locally bounded on <math id="Thmthm2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Note that since any rational symplectic manifold has full packing by an ellipsoid <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ops07</a>]</cite>, this theorem clearly implies Biran’s original result.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmdefinition4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_cor">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmdefinition4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 1.4</span></span><span id="S1.Thmdefinition4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmdefinition4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmdefinition4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.Thmdefinition4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">All closed symplectic <math id="S1.Thmdefinition4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math>-manifolds with rational symplectic class have the packing stability.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p7.1" class="ltx_p">Strong packing stability for ellipsoids does not immediately imply packing stability for all closed manifolds, however, because the following remains a conjecture.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmconj1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_conj">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmconj1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Conjecture 1</span></span><span id="Thmconj1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmconj1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmconj1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmconj1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Every (closed) <math id="Thmconj1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional symplectic manifold is fully packed by one ellipsoid.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p8.1" class="ltx_p">Nevertheless we will show that any symplectic <math id="S1.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math> manifold can be decomposed (up to a subset of volume <math id="S1.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math>) into finitely many open symplectic manifolds, each of which has a Hamiltonian toric free action with convex associated polytope, namely ellipsoids and pseudo-balls. Theorem <a href="#Thmthm1" title="Theorem 1. ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Packing stability for symplectic 4-manifolds." class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> will then follow from Theorem <a href="#Thmthm2" title="Theorem 2. ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Packing stability for symplectic 4-manifolds." class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> and the following strong packing stability for pseudo-balls.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmthm3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_thm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmthm3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 3</span></span><span id="Thmthm3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmthm3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmthm3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmthm3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The pseudo-balls have the strong packing stability. Moreover, the function <math id="Thmthm3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda(T(a,b,\alpha,\beta))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>β</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda(T(a,b,\alpha,\beta))</annotation></semantics></math> is locally bounded on <math id="Thmthm3.p1.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\{\alpha&lt;a&lt;\alpha+\beta,\beta&lt;b&lt;\alpha+\beta\}\subset\mathbb{R}^{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>β</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>β</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>β</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>4</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\alpha&lt;a&lt;\alpha+\beta,\beta&lt;b&lt;\alpha+\beta\}\subset\mathbb{R}^{4}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<section id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Related results.</h4>

<div id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx1" title="" class="ltx_ref">BH11</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx2" title="" class="ltx_ref">BH13</a>]</cite>, the first two authors proved that rational symplectic manifolds in all dimensions have packing stability. It remains an open question whether higher dimensional rational manifolds have strong packing stability. The proof argued as in Corollary <a href="#S1.Thmdefinition4" title="Corollary 1.4. ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Packing stability for symplectic 4-manifolds." class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.4</span></a> by first showing that all ellipsoids have packing stability and then remarking that any <span id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">rational</span> symplectic manifold admits a volume filling embedding from an ellipsoid. The ellipsoid packing stability ultimately relied on an embedding result for <math id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional ellipsoids.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmthmx1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_thm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmthmx1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem</span></span><span id="Thmthmx1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmthmx1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmthmx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmthmx1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">[Buse-Hind] There exists a continuous function <math id="Thmthmx1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:[1,\infty)\to[1,\infty)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:[1,\infty)\to[1,\infty)</annotation></semantics></math> such that if <math id="Thmthmx1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b&gt;f(a)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b&gt;f(a)</annotation></semantics></math> then there exists a symplectic embedding <math id="Thmthmx1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda E(1,b)\to E(1,a)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda E(1,b)\to E(1,a)</annotation></semantics></math> if any only if the volume condition is satisfied.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">This immediately implies ellipsoid packing stability in four dimensions since an ellipsoid <math id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E(1,k)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E(1,k)</annotation></semantics></math> can be fully filled by <math id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> balls. The proof of the above theorem in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx2" title="" class="ltx_ref">BH13</a>]</cite> relied on Embedded Contact Homology, although the theorem in fact also follows from the strong packing stability we establish here. Indeed, work of McDuff says that an ellipsoid embedding in dimension <math id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math> exists whenever there exists an embedding of a union of balls of appropriate sizes. It would be interesting to compare the corresponding bounds on the function <math id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Recent work of Latschev, McDuff and Schlenk in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx5" title="" class="ltx_ref">LMS12</a>]</cite> implies packing stability of <math id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional tori with linear symplectic forms (most of which are irrational). Indeed, if <math id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> is a linear <math id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional torus not a product of <math id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional tori of equal area then we have an embedding <math id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sqcup B(\lambda_{i})\overset{\omega}{\hookrightarrow}X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0em">⊔</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">𝜔</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sqcup B(\lambda_{i})\overset{\omega}{\hookrightarrow}X</annotation></semantics></math> whenever the volume obstruction <math id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{1}{2}\sum\lambda_{i}^{2}&lt;\mathrm{Vol}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>∑</mo><msubsup><mi>λ</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mi>Vol</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{1}{2}\sum\lambda_{i}^{2}&lt;\mathrm{Vol}(X)</annotation></semantics></math> is satisfied. For the product <math id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T(\mu,\mu)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T(\mu,\mu)</annotation></semantics></math> of two tori of area <math id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math> this remains true given the additional condition that <math id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{i}&lt;\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>μ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{i}&lt;\mu</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>. However whether or not there exist very full fillings of tori under just these hypotheses remains an open question.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Organization of the paper and description of methods.</h4>

<div id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As mentioned
above,
the main ingredient for our arguments is to show that two types of open symplectic manifolds, an ellipsoid and a pseudo-ball,
have strong packing stability. The conclusion of the proof of packing stability for ellipsoids (Theorem <a href="#Thmthm2" title="Theorem 2. ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Packing stability for symplectic 4-manifolds." class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>) is found in section <a href="#S3.SS3" title="3.3 Proof of theorem ‣ 3 Packing stability of ellipsoids ‣ Packing stability for symplectic 4-manifolds." class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a> and explain how the case of the pseudo-ball (Theorem <a href="#Thmthm3" title="Theorem 3. ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Packing stability for symplectic 4-manifolds." class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>) follows along the same lines in section <a href="#S4" title="4 Packing stability of pseudo-balls 𝑇(𝑎,𝑏,𝛼,𝛽) ‣ Packing stability for symplectic 4-manifolds." class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Both these results are consequences of two technical lemmas, which we deal with first. One is a basic result on packing stability of blow-ups of <math id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>,
proved in section <a href="#S2" title="2 Packing stability of rational surfaces ‣ Packing stability for symplectic 4-manifolds." class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>,
which serves as a building block for all our other packing stability results.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmdefinition5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmdefinition5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 1.5</span></span><span id="S1.Thmdefinition5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmdefinition5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmdefinition5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.Thmdefinition5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">All symplectic forms on the <math id="S1.Thmdefinition5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>-fold blow-up <math id="S1.Thmdefinition5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathbb{P}}^{2}_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>p</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathbb{P}}^{2}_{p}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S1.Thmdefinition5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> have the strong packing stability. Moreover <math id="S1.Thmdefinition5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda(\hat{\omega})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>ω</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda(\hat{\omega})</annotation></semantics></math> is bounded from below by a quantity which depends only on the volume of <math id="S1.Thmdefinition5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathbb{P}^{2}_{p},\hat{\omega})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>p</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>ω</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathbb{P}^{2}_{p},\hat{\omega})</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The second lemma,
of independent interest, allows us to isotope any packing by ellipsoids into a good position with respect to a given symplectic curve, assuming only the necessary area requirements.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmdefinition6" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmdefinition6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 1.6</span></span><span id="S1.Thmdefinition6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmdefinition6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmdefinition6.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.Thmdefinition6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S1.Thmdefinition6.p1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\sqcup_{i=1}^{k}E(a_{i},b_{i})\sqcup_{i=1}^{k^{\prime}}E(a_{i}^{\prime},b_{i}^{\prime})\sqcup_{i=1}^{k^{\prime\prime}}E(a_{i}^{\prime\prime},b_{i}^{\prime\prime})\sqcup_{i=1}^{k^{(3)}}E(a_{i}^{(3)},b_{i}^{(3)})\overset{\varphi}{\hookrightarrow}\mathbb{P}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mo>⊔</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mi>E</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msubsup><mo>⊔</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><msup><mi>k</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msubsup><mi>E</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>b</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msubsup><mo>⊔</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><msup><mi>k</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup></msubsup><mi>E</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>′′</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>b</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>′′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msubsup><mo>⊔</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><msup><mi>k</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></msubsup><mi>E</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>b</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mover accent="true"><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">𝜑</mo></mover><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\sqcup_{i=1}^{k}E(a_{i},b_{i})\sqcup_{i=1}^{k^{\prime}}E(a_{i}^{\prime},b_{i}^{\prime})\sqcup_{i=1}^{k^{\prime\prime}}E(a_{i}^{\prime\prime},b_{i}^{\prime\prime})\sqcup_{i=1}^{k^{(3)}}E(a_{i}^{(3)},b_{i}^{(3)})\overset{\varphi}{\hookrightarrow}\mathbb{P}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> be a symplectic embedding, which is the restriction of an embedding of the disjoint union of closed ellipsoids, and let <math id="S1.Thmdefinition6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> an immersed,
possibly reducible,
symplectic curve with positive self-intersections only.
We denote by <math id="S1.Thmdefinition6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> the ellipsoid <math id="S1.Thmdefinition6.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E(a_{i},b_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E(a_{i},b_{i})</annotation></semantics></math>, by <math id="S1.Thmdefinition6.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E^{\prime}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>E</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E^{\prime}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> the ellipsoid <math id="S1.Thmdefinition6.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E(a_{i}^{\prime},b_{i}^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>b</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E(a_{i}^{\prime},b_{i}^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> and so on.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmdefinition6.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmdefinition6.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.Thmdefinition6.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let us assign a component of <math id="S1.Thmdefinition6.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> to each ellipsoid <math id="S1.Thmdefinition6.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E(a_{i},b_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E(a_{i},b_{i})</annotation></semantics></math> and to each <math id="S1.Thmdefinition6.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E(a_{i}^{\prime},b_{i}^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>b</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E(a_{i}^{\prime},b_{i}^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math>. Suppose we can also assign a self-intersection point of <math id="S1.Thmdefinition6.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> to each ellipsoid <math id="S1.Thmdefinition6.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E(a_{i}^{\prime\prime},b_{i}^{\prime\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>′′</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>b</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>′′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E(a_{i}^{\prime\prime},b_{i}^{\prime\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> together with a different branch for the two axes of the ellipsoids. Assume that each component of <math id="S1.Thmdefinition6.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> has area strictly greater than the sum of all <math id="S1.Thmdefinition6.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{p}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S1.Thmdefinition6.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b_{q}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>b</mi><mi>q</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_{q}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S1.Thmdefinition6.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{r}^{\prime\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>′′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{r}^{\prime\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.Thmdefinition6.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b_{s}^{\prime\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>b</mi><mi>s</mi><mo>′′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_{s}^{\prime\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> for which <math id="S1.Thmdefinition6.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E(a_{p},b_{p})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E(a_{p},b_{p})</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S1.Thmdefinition6.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E(a_{q}^{\prime},b_{q}^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mi>q</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>b</mi><mi>q</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E(a_{q}^{\prime},b_{q}^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math>, the first axis of <math id="S1.Thmdefinition6.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E(a_{r}^{\prime\prime},b_{r}^{\prime\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>′′</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>b</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>′′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E(a_{r}^{\prime\prime},b_{r}^{\prime\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> and the second axis of <math id="S1.Thmdefinition6.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E(a_{s}^{\prime\prime},b_{s}^{\prime\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mi>s</mi><mo>′′</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>b</mi><mi>s</mi><mo>′′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E(a_{s}^{\prime\prime},b_{s}^{\prime\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> are assigned to it.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmdefinition6.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmdefinition6.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.Thmdefinition6.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Then <math id="S1.Thmdefinition6.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> is symplectically isotopic to a <math id="S1.Thmdefinition6.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> with the following properties:</span></p>
<table id="S1.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{array}[]{l}{E}_{i}\cap C^{\prime}=\varphi(\{w=0\})=\varphi(\mathbb{D}(a_{i})\times\{0\}),\\
{E}_{i}^{\prime}\cap C^{\prime}=\varphi(\{z=0\})=\varphi(\{0\}\times\mathbb{D}(b_{i}^{\prime}))\\
{E}_{i}^{\prime\prime}\cap C^{\prime}=\varphi(\{z=0\}\cup\{w=0\})=\varphi(\mathbb{D}(a_{i}^{\prime\prime})\times\{0\}\cup\{0\}\times\mathbb{D}(b_{i}^{\prime\prime}))\\
{E}_{i}^{(3)}\cap C^{\prime}=\emptyset\end{array}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mi>E</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∩</mo><mi>C</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mi>φ</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>w</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>φ</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝔻</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mi>E</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>∩</mo><mi>C</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mi>φ</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>φ</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">}</mo><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>𝔻</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>b</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mi>E</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>′′</mo></msup><mo>∩</mo><mi>C</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mi>φ</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo><mo>∪</mo><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>w</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>φ</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝔻</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>′′</mo></msup><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo><mo>∪</mo><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">}</mo><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>𝔻</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>b</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>′′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>E</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>∩</mo><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{array}[]{l}{E}_{i}\cap C^{\prime}=\varphi(\{w=0\})=\varphi(\mathbb{D}(a_{i})\times\{0\}),\\
{E}_{i}^{\prime}\cap C^{\prime}=\varphi(\{z=0\})=\varphi(\{0\}\times\mathbb{D}(b_{i}^{\prime}))\\
{E}_{i}^{\prime\prime}\cap C^{\prime}=\varphi(\{z=0\}\cup\{w=0\})=\varphi(\mathbb{D}(a_{i}^{\prime\prime})\times\{0\}\cup\{0\}\times\mathbb{D}(b_{i}^{\prime\prime}))\\
{E}_{i}^{(3)}\cap C^{\prime}=\emptyset\end{array}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">We prove this lemma in section <a href="#S3.SS2" title="3.2 Ellipsoid embeddings in good positions relative to a given curve ‣ 3 Packing stability of ellipsoids ‣ Packing stability for symplectic 4-manifolds." class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>, after recalling some results of McDuff relating ellipsoid and ball packings in section <a href="#S3.SS1" title="3.1 Background results on ellipsoid embeddings ‣ 3 Packing stability of ellipsoids ‣ Packing stability for symplectic 4-manifolds." class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmdefinition7" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_rk">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmdefinition7.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 1.7</span></span><span id="S1.Thmdefinition7.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmdefinition7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmdefinition7.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.Thmdefinition7.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Although this lemma definitely uses the fact that <math id="S1.Thmdefinition7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S1.Thmdefinition7.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>J</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J</annotation></semantics></math>-holomorphic for an <math id="S1.Thmdefinition7.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math>-compatible <math id="S1.Thmdefinition7.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>J</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J</annotation></semantics></math>, it does not seem to be a purely pseudo-holomorphic statement. The lemma applies in cases when our <math id="S1.Thmdefinition7.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>J</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J</annotation></semantics></math>-curves have very negative index, and can therefore be found only in very large-dimensional families of almost complex structure. This makes
the task of finding isotopies of these curves via pseudo-holomorphic methods rather hopeless.
The proof relies instead on the singular inflation technique, and what might be surprising is that it shows that this statement belongs to the soft side of symplectic geometry.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Given these preliminaries, Theorem <a href="#Thmthm1" title="Theorem 1. ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Packing stability for symplectic 4-manifolds." class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> is put together in section <a href="#S5" title="5 Packing stability of irrational closed symplectic 4-manifolds ‣ Packing stability for symplectic 4-manifolds." class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>. In section <a href="#S5.SS1" title="5.1 Decomposition of symplectic 4-manifolds with irrational symplectic class ‣ 5 Packing stability of irrational closed symplectic 4-manifolds ‣ Packing stability for symplectic 4-manifolds." class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.1</span></a> we describe the decomposition of (irrational) symplectic manifolds into a finite number of ellipsoids and pseudo-balls. Applying the packing stability for these open manifolds, we complete the proof of Theorem <a href="#Thmthm1" title="Theorem 1. ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Packing stability for symplectic 4-manifolds." class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> in section <a href="#S5.SS2" title="5.2 Proof of theorem ‣ 5 Packing stability of irrational closed symplectic 4-manifolds ‣ Packing stability for symplectic 4-manifolds." class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.2</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Packing stability of rational surfaces</h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The aim of this section is to explain lemma <a href="#S1.Thmdefinition5" title="Lemma 1.5. ‣ Organization of the paper and description of methods. ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Packing stability for symplectic 4-manifolds." class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.5</span></a>. We recall the following result by Biran <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bir97</a>]</cite>.
Let <math id="S2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\hat{\mathbb{P}}^{2}_{p},\hat{\omega})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>p</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>ω</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\hat{\mathbb{P}}^{2}_{p},\hat{\omega})</annotation></semantics></math> be a symplectic blow-up of <math id="S2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, and call <math id="S2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega:=[\hat{\omega}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>ω</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega:=[\hat{\omega}]</annotation></semantics></math> the cohomology class of <math id="S2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\omega}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>ω</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\omega}</annotation></semantics></math>. We normalize so that lines in <math id="S2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> have area <math id="S2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>. Define</p>
<table id="S2.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{\Omega}:=\inf\left\{\frac{\Omega(B)}{c_{1}(B)},\;\text{ for }B^{2}\geq 0,\;\Omega(B)&gt;0,\;c_{1}(B)\geq 2\right\}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.1389em">:=</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0.1389em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">inf</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo rspace="0.447em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mtext> for </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.447em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.447em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{\Omega}:=\inf\left\{\frac{\Omega(B)}{c_{1}(B)},\;\text{ for }B^{2}\geq 0,\;\Omega(B)&gt;0,\;c_{1}(B)\geq 2\right\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="Thmthmx2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_thm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmthmx2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem</span></span><span id="Thmthmx2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Biran)<span id="Thmthmx2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmthmx2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmthmx2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmthmx2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The quantity <math id="Thmthmx2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{\Omega}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{\Omega}</annotation></semantics></math> bounds <math id="Thmthmx2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda(\hat{\mathbb{P}}^{2}_{p},\hat{\omega})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>p</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>ω</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda(\hat{\mathbb{P}}^{2}_{p},\hat{\omega})</annotation></semantics></math> from below.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Of course, this theorem is interesting only when <math id="S2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{\Omega}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{\Omega}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>. Lemma <a href="#S1.Thmdefinition5" title="Lemma 1.5. ‣ Organization of the paper and description of methods. ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Packing stability for symplectic 4-manifolds." class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.5</span></a> will therefore follow if we can bound <math id="S2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{\Omega}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{\Omega}</annotation></semantics></math> from below by a quantity which depends only on the volume of <math id="S2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\hat{\mathbb{P}}^{2}_{p},\hat{\omega})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>p</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>ω</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\hat{\mathbb{P}}^{2}_{p},\hat{\omega})</annotation></semantics></math>. This is precisely the content of the following lemma.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmdefinition1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmdefinition1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 2.1</span></span><span id="S2.Thmdefinition1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmdefinition1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmdefinition1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmdefinition1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S2.Thmdefinition1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\omega}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>ω</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\omega}</annotation></semantics></math> be a symplectic form on <math id="S2.Thmdefinition1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathbb{P}}^{2}_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>p</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathbb{P}}^{2}_{p}</annotation></semantics></math> obtained by blowing-up <math id="S2.Thmdefinition1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> balls of sizes <math id="S2.Thmdefinition1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\lambda_{1},\dots,\lambda_{p})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\lambda_{1},\dots,\lambda_{p})</annotation></semantics></math>. Denoting by <math id="S2.Thmdefinition1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa:=\sqrt{\sum\lambda_{i}^{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo>:=</mo><msqrt><mrow><mo>∑</mo><msubsup><mi>λ</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa:=\sqrt{\sum\lambda_{i}^{2}}</annotation></semantics></math>, we have</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{\Omega}\geq\frac{1-\kappa}{3+\sqrt{p}}=\frac{1-\sqrt{1-2\mathrm{Vol}(\hat{\mathbb{P}}^{2}_{p})}}{3+\sqrt{p}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi></msub><mo>≥</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>κ</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>+</mo><msqrt><mi>p</mi></msqrt></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>V</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>p</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msqrt></mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>+</mo><msqrt><mi>p</mi></msqrt></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{\Omega}\geq\frac{1-\kappa}{3+\sqrt{p}}=\frac{1-\sqrt{1-2\mathrm{Vol}(\hat{\mathbb{P}}^{2}_{p})}}{3+\sqrt{p}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Note that to be able to blow-up balls of these sizes we automatically have <math id="S2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa&lt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa&lt;1</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Proof:</span> Let <math id="S2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B\in H_{2}(\hat{\mathbb{P}}^{2}_{p})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>p</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B\in H_{2}(\hat{\mathbb{P}}^{2}_{p})</annotation></semantics></math>, decomposed in the following way:</p>
<table id="S2.Ex5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B=kL-\sum m_{i}E_{i}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">−</mo><mrow><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B=kL-\sum m_{i}E_{i},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p4.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L,E_{1},\dots,E_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L,E_{1},\dots,E_{p}</annotation></semantics></math> is the standard basis of <math id="S2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{2}(\hat{\mathbb{P}}^{2}_{p})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>p</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{2}(\hat{\mathbb{P}}^{2}_{p})</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S2.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k,m_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k,m_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> are integers.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">The condition <math id="S2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B^{2}\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B^{2}\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math> implies that <math id="S2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k^{2}-\sum m_{i}^{2}\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo rspace="0.055em">−</mo><mrow><mo>∑</mo><msubsup><mi>m</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k^{2}-\sum m_{i}^{2}\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math>, hence</p>
<table id="S2.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum m_{i}^{2}\leq k^{2}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><msubsup><mi>m</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo>≤</mo><msup><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum m_{i}^{2}\leq k^{2}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S2.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Hence by Cauchy-Schwartz inequality, for a class with <math id="S2.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B^{2}\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B^{2}\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math> we have</p>
<table id="S2.Ex6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left|\sum m_{i}\lambda_{i}\right|\leq\sqrt{\sum m_{i}^{2}}\sqrt{\sum\lambda_{i}^{2}}=\kappa\sqrt{\sum m_{i}^{2}}&lt;\sqrt{\sum m_{i}^{2}}\leq|k|." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>|</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em" movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>|</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><msubsup><mi>m</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></msqrt><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msqrt><mrow><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><msubsup><mi>λ</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></msqrt></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msqrt><mrow><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><msubsup><mi>m</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></msqrt></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><msqrt><mrow><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><msubsup><mi>m</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></msqrt><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left|\sum m_{i}\lambda_{i}\right|\leq\sqrt{\sum m_{i}^{2}}\sqrt{\sum\lambda_{i}^{2}}=\kappa\sqrt{\sum m_{i}^{2}}&lt;\sqrt{\sum m_{i}^{2}}\leq|k|.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S2.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p7.1" class="ltx_p">Now <math id="S2.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega(B)=k-\sum m_{i}\lambda_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo rspace="0.055em">−</mo><mrow><mo>∑</mo><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega(B)=k-\sum m_{i}\lambda_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>.
We therefore see that under the conditions <math id="S2.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B^{2}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B^{2}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega(B)&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega(B)&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> we have <math id="S2.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega(B)\leq k+|k|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega(B)\leq k+|k|</annotation></semantics></math> and hence <math id="S2.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> and, by the same Cauchy-Schwarz inequality</p>
<table id="S2.Ex7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega(B)&gt;k(1-\kappa)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>κ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega(B)&gt;k(1-\kappa).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S2.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p8.1" class="ltx_p">Finally, for a class with <math id="S2.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B^{2}\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B^{2}\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega(B)&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega(B)&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>,</p>
<table id="S2.Ex8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{1}(B)=3k-\sum m_{i}\leq 3k+\sqrt{p}\sqrt{\sum m_{i}^{2}}\leq 3k+\sqrt{p}k." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">−</mo><mrow><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msqrt><mi>p</mi></msqrt><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msqrt><mrow><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><msubsup><mi>m</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msqrt><mi>p</mi></msqrt><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{1}(B)=3k-\sum m_{i}\leq 3k+\sqrt{p}\sqrt{\sum m_{i}^{2}}\leq 3k+\sqrt{p}k.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p8.2" class="ltx_p">Putting these inequalities together, we get:</p>
<table id="S2.Ex9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{\Omega}\geq\frac{(1-\kappa)k}{(3+\sqrt{p})k}=\frac{1-\kappa}{3+\sqrt{p}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi></msub><mo>≥</mo><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>κ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>+</mo><msqrt><mi>p</mi></msqrt></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>κ</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>+</mo><msqrt><mi>p</mi></msqrt></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{\Omega}\geq\frac{(1-\kappa)k}{(3+\sqrt{p})k}=\frac{1-\kappa}{3+\sqrt{p}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p8.3" class="ltx_p"><math id="S2.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\square" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">□</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\square</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Packing stability of ellipsoids</h2>

<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>Background results on ellipsoid embeddings</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We recall two facts on embedding of ellipsoids. The first one, stated in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx7" title="" class="ltx_ref">McD09</a>]</cite>, relates ellipsoid embeddings to ball packings.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmthm4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_thm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmthm4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 4</span></span><span id="Thmthm4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(McDuff, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx7" title="" class="ltx_ref">McD09</a>]</cite>)<span id="Thmthm4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmthm4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmthm4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmthm4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For each <math id="Thmthm4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a\in\mathbb{Q}^{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℚ</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a\in\mathbb{Q}^{+}</annotation></semantics></math>, there is an in integer <math id="Thmthm4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(a)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(a)</annotation></semantics></math> and weights <math id="Thmthm4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf w}(a):=(w_{1}(a),\dots,w_{p(a)}(a))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf w}(a):=(w_{1}(a),\dots,w_{p(a)}(a))</annotation></semantics></math>, such that for all rational symplectic <math id="Thmthm4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math>-manifolds <math id="Thmthm4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(X,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(X,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="Thmthm4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau E(1,a)\overset{\omega}{\hookrightarrow}X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">𝜔</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau E(1,a)\overset{\omega}{\hookrightarrow}X</annotation></semantics></math> if and only if <math id="Thmthm4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sqcup\tau B(w_{i})\overset{\omega}{\hookrightarrow}X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0em">⊔</mo><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">𝜔</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sqcup\tau B(w_{i})\overset{\omega}{\hookrightarrow}X</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The second one follows readily from the proof of the previous theorem, in particular the construction of the <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{i}(a)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{i}(a)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmdefinition1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmdefinition1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 3.1</span></span><span id="S3.Thmdefinition1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmdefinition1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmdefinition1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmdefinition1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Fix <math id="S3.Thmdefinition1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a\in\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a\in\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math>. There exists <math id="S3.Thmdefinition1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ε</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> (that depends on <math id="S3.Thmdefinition1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math>), such that for all <math id="S3.Thmdefinition1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon&lt;\varepsilon_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ε</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>ε</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon&lt;\varepsilon_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.Thmdefinition1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>a</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S3.Thmdefinition1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|a^{\prime}-a|&lt;\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msup><mi>a</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>−</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|a^{\prime}-a|&lt;\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.Thmdefinition1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p^{\prime}(a)&gt;p(a)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>p</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p^{\prime}(a)&gt;p(a)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.Thmdefinition1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf w}(a^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>a</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf w}(a^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> verifies the following:</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{array}[]{ll}|w_{i}(a^{\prime})-w_{i}(a)|&lt;\varepsilon&amp;\forall i\leq p,\\
|w_{i}(a^{\prime})|&lt;\varepsilon&amp;\forall i&gt;p.\end{array}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>a</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>a</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{array}[]{ll}|w_{i}(a^{\prime})-w_{i}(a)|&lt;\varepsilon&amp;\forall i\leq p,\\
|w_{i}(a^{\prime})|&lt;\varepsilon&amp;\forall i&gt;p.\end{array}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We will need to apply not only Theorem <a href="#Thmthm4" title="Theorem 4 (McDuff, []). ‣ 3.1 Background results on ellipsoid embeddings ‣ 3 Packing stability of ellipsoids ‣ Packing stability for symplectic 4-manifolds." class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>, but also the blowing up procedure in a neighborhood of an ellipsoid which it relies on. Therefore we briefly recall this procedure now, following <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx7" title="" class="ltx_ref">McD09</a>]</cite>, section <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">First of all, the number of steps in a continued fraction expansion gives us a function <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p:\mathbb{Q}^{+}\to\mathbb{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℚ</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p:\mathbb{Q}^{+}\to\mathbb{N}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then McDuff shows that corresponding to each rational ellipsoid <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E=E(a,b)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E=E(a,b)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>-tuple <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta\in\mathbb{R}^{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>p</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta\in\mathbb{R}^{p}</annotation></semantics></math> of small (admissible) numbers, where <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p=p(b/a)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p=p(b/a)</annotation></semantics></math>, there is a sequence of symplectic spheres <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{1},\dots,S_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{1},\dots,S_{p}</annotation></semantics></math> which are <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>J</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J</annotation></semantics></math>-holomorphic for a tame almost-complex structure on a suitable <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>-fold blow-up of <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{C}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{C}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>. The complement of <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\cup S_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0em">∪</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\cup S_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> in a small neighborhood <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\cup S_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0em">∪</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\cup S_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> is symplectomorphic to a neighborhood of the boundary in a carefully chosen but arbitrarily small domain containing <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E\subset\mathbb{C}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E\subset\mathbb{C}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>. In other words, an arbitrarily small neighborhood of <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>E</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E</annotation></semantics></math> can be replaced by a configuration of <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>J</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J</annotation></semantics></math>-holomorphic curves, and conversely, by a version of Weinstein’s Theorem, given any such configuration of curves where the components have the required areas and relative intersections, we can remove a small neighborhood and replace it with a neighborhood of our ellipsoid <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>E</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">The sequence of curves <math id="S3.SS1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> is constructed recursively starting with a neighborhood <math id="S3.SS1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{0}\subset\mathbb{C}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{0}\subset\mathbb{C}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S3.SS1.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>E</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E</annotation></semantics></math> which is defined in terms of the choice of <math id="S3.SS1.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math>. The precise algorithm for the blowing-up can be found in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx7" title="" class="ltx_ref">McD09</a>]</cite>, here we just outline briefly how the singular set arises.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Step <math id="S3.SS1.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>.</span> At the first stage we blow-up a ball of size <math id="S3.SS1.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{1}=aw_{1}(b/a)+\delta_{1}=a+\delta_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{1}=aw_{1}(b/a)+\delta_{1}=a+\delta_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> inside <math id="S3.SS1.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>. The ball intersects the short axis, say <math id="S3.SS1.p6.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\{z_{2}=0\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{z_{2}=0\}</annotation></semantics></math>, of <math id="S3.SS1.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>E</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E</annotation></semantics></math> along a complex line and so the blow-up <math id="S3.SS1.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> contains an exceptional divisor <math id="S3.SS1.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>E</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and the proper transform <math id="S3.SS1.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S3.SS1.p6.m9" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\{z_{2}=0\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{z_{2}=0\}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Step <math id="S3.SS1.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>.</span> Now we blow-up a ball of size <math id="S3.SS1.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{2}=aw_{2}(b/a)+\delta_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{2}=aw_{2}(b/a)+\delta_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> inside <math id="S3.SS1.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>. This ball will intersect <math id="S3.SS1.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>E</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.p7.m5" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\{z_{1}=0\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{z_{1}=0\}</annotation></semantics></math> along complex lines while avoiding <math id="S3.SS1.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus the blow-up <math id="S3.SS1.p7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> will contain an exceptional divisor <math id="S3.SS1.p7.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>E</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, the proper transform of <math id="S3.SS1.p7.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>E</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, the proper transform <math id="S3.SS1.p7.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S3.SS1.p7.m11" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\{z_{1}=0\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{z_{1}=0\}</annotation></semantics></math> and still <math id="S3.SS1.p7.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p8.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Step <math id="S3.SS1.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>.</span> Again we blow-up a ball of a certain size <math id="S3.SS1.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{i}=aw_{i}(b/a)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{i}=aw_{i}(b/a)</annotation></semantics></math> to produce a blow-up <math id="S3.SS1.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S3.SS1.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{i-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>U</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{i-1}</annotation></semantics></math> with an exceptional divisor <math id="S3.SS1.p8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>. This ball will intersect exactly two of the curves we have considered previously, that is, proper transforms of <math id="S3.SS1.p8.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS1.p8.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S3.SS1.p8.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S3.SS1.p8.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j&lt;i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j&lt;i</annotation></semantics></math>, and will intersect these along complex lines.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p9.1" class="ltx_p">The construction is performed so that the volume of <math id="S3.SS1.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{p}</annotation></semantics></math> is of order <math id="S3.SS1.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math> and all points originally in the ellipsoid <math id="S3.SS1.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>E</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E</annotation></semantics></math> eventually lie in one of the balls to be blown-up. The curve <math id="S3.SS1.p9.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> is the proper transform of <math id="S3.SS1.p9.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus its self-intersection is <math id="S3.SS1.p9.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-1</annotation></semantics></math> minus the number of subsequent balls which intersect the transform of <math id="S3.SS1.p9.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>. The construction is such that <math id="S3.SS1.p9.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{p}=E_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{p}=E_{p}</annotation></semantics></math> is the only curve with intersection <math id="S3.SS1.p9.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-1</annotation></semantics></math> and the <math id="S3.SS1.p9.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S3.SS1.p9.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j&lt;p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j&lt;p</annotation></semantics></math> all have area of order <math id="S3.SS1.p9.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p10.1" class="ltx_p">The proper transforms of <math id="S3.SS1.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> will each intersect exactly one of the <math id="S3.SS1.p10.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>, corresponding to the last ball which intersects the transform of the planes <math id="S3.SS1.p10.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\{z_{2}=0\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{z_{2}=0\}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.p10.m5" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\{z_{1}=0\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{z_{1}=0\}</annotation></semantics></math> respectively. When we reverse the construction we may assume that any given holomorphic curve which intersects this <math id="S3.SS1.p10.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> in a single point transversally is blown-down to a curve intersecting our neighborhood of <math id="S3.SS1.p10.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>E</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E</annotation></semantics></math> in the corresponding axis.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2 </span>Ellipsoid embeddings in good positions relative to a given curve</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section we give the proof of Lemma <a href="#S1.Thmdefinition6" title="Lemma 1.6. ‣ Organization of the paper and description of methods. ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Packing stability for symplectic 4-manifolds." class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.6</span></a>, and follow that notation. First note that since we start with embeddings of closed ellipsoids, and since the area inequalities for components of <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> are strict, the embeddings extend to disjoint rational ellipsoids which still satisfy the inequalities. Hence we may assume that all of our ellipsoids are rational. Moreover when choosing parameters <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math> as in section <a href="#S3.SS1" title="3.1 Background results on ellipsoid embeddings ‣ 3 Packing stability of ellipsoids ‣ Packing stability for symplectic 4-manifolds." class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a> we will assume that the embeddings of disjoint closed ellipsoids extend to embeddings of disjoint neighborhoods of size <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math>.
As in section <a href="#S2" title="2 Packing stability of rational surfaces ‣ Packing stability for symplectic 4-manifolds." class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> we normalize so that lines in <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> have area <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We need to show that <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> is symplectically isotopic to a curve <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> in a special position with respect to the ellipsoids. Since in <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, ellipsoid packings are all isotopic, see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx9" title="" class="ltx_ref">MO13</a>]</cite>, we can alternatively prove that there exists a packing by the same ellipsoids which is in the desired position with respect to <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> (we will say for short that our packing is <span id="S3.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">directed</span> by the curve <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Our hypotheses on the existence of intersection points together with Darboux’s theorem implies the existence at least of directed symplectic embeddings of the union <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sqcup\tau E_{i}\sqcup\tau E^{\prime}_{i}\sqcup\tau E^{\prime\prime}_{i}\sqcup\tau E^{(3)}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0em">⊔</mo><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>⊔</mo><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>E</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo>⊔</mo><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>E</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>′′</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo>⊔</mo><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>E</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sqcup\tau E_{i}\sqcup\tau E^{\prime}_{i}\sqcup\tau E^{\prime\prime}_{i}\sqcup\tau E^{(3)}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> sufficiently small.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">We then blow-up the scaled ellipsoids (or rather the correct neighbourhood of the ellipsoids), as described in section <a href="#S3.SS1" title="3.1 Background results on ellipsoid embeddings ‣ 3 Packing stability of ellipsoids ‣ Packing stability for symplectic 4-manifolds." class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>. This provides a symplectic form <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\omega}_{\tau}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>ω</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>τ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\omega}_{\tau}</annotation></semantics></math> on the <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math>-fold blow-up <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathbb{P}}^{2}_{P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathbb{P}}^{2}_{P}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, where</p>
<table id="S3.Ex11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P=\sum_{i=1}^{k}p\big(\frac{b_{i}}{a_{i}}\big)+\sum_{i=1}^{k^{\prime}}p\big(\frac{b^{\prime}_{i}}{a^{\prime}_{i}}\big)+\sum_{i=1}^{k^{\prime\prime}}p\big(\frac{b^{\prime\prime}_{i}}{a^{\prime\prime}_{i}}\big)+\sum_{i=1}^{k^{(3)}}p\big(\frac{b^{(3)}_{i}}{a^{(3)}_{i}}\big)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></munderover><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mfrac><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mfrac><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">+</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><msup><mi>k</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></munderover><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mfrac><msubsup><mi>b</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mfrac><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">+</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><msup><mi>k</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup></munderover><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mfrac><msubsup><mi>b</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>′′</mo></msubsup><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>′′</mo></msubsup></mfrac><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">+</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><msup><mi>k</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></munderover><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mfrac><msubsup><mi>b</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mfrac><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P=\sum_{i=1}^{k}p\big(\frac{b_{i}}{a_{i}}\big)+\sum_{i=1}^{k^{\prime}}p\big(\frac{b^{\prime}_{i}}{a^{\prime}_{i}}\big)+\sum_{i=1}^{k^{\prime\prime}}p\big(\frac{b^{\prime\prime}_{i}}{a^{\prime\prime}_{i}}\big)+\sum_{i=1}^{k^{(3)}}p\big(\frac{b^{(3)}_{i}}{a^{(3)}_{i}}\big),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p4.2" class="ltx_p">and <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> is the integer function that appears in Theorem <a href="#Thmthm4" title="Theorem 4 (McDuff, []). ‣ 3.1 Background results on ellipsoid embeddings ‣ 3 Packing stability of ellipsoids ‣ Packing stability for symplectic 4-manifolds." class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> above. Write <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{i}=p(\frac{b_{i}}{a_{i}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mfrac><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mfrac><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{i}=p(\frac{b_{i}}{a_{i}})</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p^{\prime}_{i}=p(\frac{b^{\prime}_{i}}{a^{\prime}_{i}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mfrac><msubsup><mi>b</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mfrac><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p^{\prime}_{i}=p(\frac{b^{\prime}_{i}}{a^{\prime}_{i}})</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p^{\prime\prime}_{i}=p(\frac{b^{\prime\prime}_{i}}{a^{\prime\prime}_{i}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>′′</mo></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mfrac><msubsup><mi>b</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>′′</mo></msubsup><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>′′</mo></msubsup></mfrac><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p^{\prime\prime}_{i}=p(\frac{b^{\prime\prime}_{i}}{a^{\prime\prime}_{i}})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p^{(3)}_{i}=p(\frac{b^{(3)}_{i}}{a^{(3)}_{i}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mfrac><msubsup><mi>b</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mfrac><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p^{(3)}_{i}=p(\frac{b^{(3)}_{i}}{a^{(3)}_{i}})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">The symplectic form comes with a singular set <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}</annotation></semantics></math> (in the sense of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx9" title="" class="ltx_ref">MO13</a>]</cite>): disjoint bunches of embedded negative spheres <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}^{\prime}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}^{\prime}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}^{\prime\prime}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>′′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}^{\prime\prime}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}^{(3)}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}^{(3)}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> corresponding to each ellipsoid as in section <a href="#S3.SS1" title="3.1 Background results on ellipsoid embeddings ‣ 3 Packing stability of ellipsoids ‣ Packing stability for symplectic 4-manifolds." class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>. We denote the spheres in <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> by <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{i,j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{i,j}</annotation></semantics></math>, the spheres in <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}^{\prime}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}^{\prime}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> by <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S^{\prime}_{i,j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S^{\prime}_{i,j}</annotation></semantics></math> and so on. Also, we denote the exceptional classes corresponding to the <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau E_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau E_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> by <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[F_{i,j}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[F_{i,j}]</annotation></semantics></math>, that is, <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[F_{i,j}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[F_{i,j}]</annotation></semantics></math> is the class of the exceptional divisor arising from the <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math>th blow-up of a neighborhood of the embedded <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau E_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau E_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>. Similarly define <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[F^{\prime}_{i,j}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msubsup><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[F^{\prime}_{i,j}]</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[F^{\prime\prime}_{i,j}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msubsup><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>′′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[F^{\prime\prime}_{i,j}]</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[F^{(3)}_{i,j}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msubsup><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[F^{(3)}_{i,j}]</annotation></semantics></math>. Then with respect to the form <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\omega}_{\tau}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>ω</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>τ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\omega}_{\tau}</annotation></semantics></math> we may assume that the class <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[F_{i,j}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[F_{i,j}]</annotation></semantics></math> has area <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau k_{i,j}=\tau(a_{i}w_{i}(b_{i}/a_{i})+\delta_{i,j})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau k_{i,j}=\tau(a_{i}w_{i}(b_{i}/a_{i})+\delta_{i,j})</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[F^{\prime}_{i,j}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msubsup><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[F^{\prime}_{i,j}]</annotation></semantics></math> has area <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau k^{\prime}_{i,j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>k</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau k^{\prime}_{i,j}</annotation></semantics></math> and so on for the other ellipsoids.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p6.1" class="ltx_p">The blow-up <math id="S3.SS2.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathbb{P}}^{2}_{P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathbb{P}}^{2}_{P}</annotation></semantics></math> also contains the strict transform of <math id="S3.SS2.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math>, denoted <math id="S3.SS2.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{C}</annotation></semantics></math>. As <math id="S3.SS2.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> intersects the ellipsoids along their axes (if at all) we recall again from the previous section that this implies <math id="S3.SS2.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{C}</annotation></semantics></math> intersects transversally, <math id="S3.SS2.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math>-orthogonally, exactly one of the spheres <math id="S3.SS2.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{i,j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{i,j}</annotation></semantics></math>, exactly one of the spheres <math id="S3.SS2.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S^{\prime}_{i,j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S^{\prime}_{i,j}</annotation></semantics></math>, two of the spheres <math id="S3.SS2.p6.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S^{\prime\prime}_{i,j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>′′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S^{\prime\prime}_{i,j}</annotation></semantics></math> but avoids the <math id="S3.SS2.p6.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S^{(3)}_{i,j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S^{(3)}_{i,j}</annotation></semantics></math>. As a consequence <math id="S3.SS2.p6.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}:=\mathcal{S}\cup C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo>∪</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}:=\mathcal{S}\cup C</annotation></semantics></math> is a singular set in the sense of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx9" title="" class="ltx_ref">MO13</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p7.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S3.SS2.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> denote the class of a line in <math id="S3.SS2.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, and also of the same line in <math id="S3.SS2.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathbb{P}}^{2}_{P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathbb{P}}^{2}_{P}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmdefinition2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmdefinition2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 3.2</span></span><span id="S3.Thmdefinition2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmdefinition2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmdefinition2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmdefinition2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">There exists a symplectic packing of <math id="S3.Thmdefinition2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sqcup E_{i}\sqcup E^{\prime}_{i}\sqcup E^{\prime\prime}_{i}\sqcup E^{(3)}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0em">⊔</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>⊔</mo><msubsup><mi>E</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>⊔</mo><msubsup><mi>E</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>′′</mo></msubsup><mo>⊔</mo><msubsup><mi>E</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sqcup E_{i}\sqcup E^{\prime}_{i}\sqcup E^{\prime\prime}_{i}\sqcup E^{(3)}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, directed by <math id="S3.Thmdefinition2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math>, if there exists <math id="S3.Thmdefinition2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N\in\mathbb{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N\in\mathbb{N}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.Thmdefinition2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> and a connected embedded symplectic curve <math id="S3.Thmdefinition2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S3.Thmdefinition2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q^{2}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>Q</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q^{2}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> in the class</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A=N(L-(1+\delta)\sum_{i,j}k_{i,j}[F_{i,j}]-(1+\delta)\sum_{i,j}k^{\prime}_{i,j}[F^{\prime}_{i,j}]" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><msubsup><mi>k</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msubsup><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em">CLOSE</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A=N(L-(1+\delta)\sum_{i,j}k_{i,j}[F_{i,j}]-(1+\delta)\sum_{i,j}k^{\prime}_{i,j}[F^{\prime}_{i,j}]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<table id="S3.Ex13" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex13.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="-(1+\delta)\sum_{i,j}k^{\prime\prime}_{i,j}[F^{\prime\prime}_{i,j}]-(1+\delta)\sum_{i,j}k^{(3)}_{i,j}[F^{(3)}_{i,j}])," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></munder><msubsup><mi>k</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>′′</mo></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msubsup><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>′′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></munder><msubsup><mi>k</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msubsup><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-(1+\delta)\sum_{i,j}k^{\prime\prime}_{i,j}[F^{\prime\prime}_{i,j}]-(1+\delta)\sum_{i,j}k^{(3)}_{i,j}[F^{(3)}_{i,j}]),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.Thmdefinition2.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmdefinition2.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">which intersects <math id="S3.Thmdefinition2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}</annotation></semantics></math> positively and transversally.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.1" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S3.SS2.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p8.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text">As <math id="S3.SS2.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q^{2}\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>Q</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q^{2}\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math> we can inflate by adding a tubular neighborhood of <math id="S3.SS2.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math> with fibers of any area <math id="S3.SS2.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>. The result will be a symplectic form Poincaré dual to</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex14" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1+rN)L-\sum_{i,j}(\tau+Nr(1+\delta))k_{i,j}[F_{i,j}]-\sum_{i,j}(\tau+Nr(1+\delta))k^{\prime}_{i,j}[F^{\prime}_{i,j}]" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">−</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">−</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>k</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msubsup><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1+rN)L-\sum_{i,j}(\tau+Nr(1+\delta))k_{i,j}[F_{i,j}]-\sum_{i,j}(\tau+Nr(1+\delta))k^{\prime}_{i,j}[F^{\prime}_{i,j}]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<table id="S3.Ex15" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex15.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="-\sum_{i,j}(\tau+Nr(1+\delta))k^{\prime\prime}_{i,j}[F^{\prime\prime}_{i,j}]-\sum_{i,j}(\tau+Nr(1+\delta))k^{(3)}_{i,j}[F^{(3)}_{i,j}]." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">−</mo><munder><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>N</mi><mi>r</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msubsup><mi>k</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>′′</mo></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msubsup><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>′′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">−</mo><munder><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>N</mi><mi>r</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msubsup><mi>k</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msubsup><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-\sum_{i,j}(\tau+Nr(1+\delta))k^{\prime\prime}_{i,j}[F^{\prime\prime}_{i,j}]-\sum_{i,j}(\tau+Nr(1+\delta))k^{(3)}_{i,j}[F^{(3)}_{i,j}].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p9.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p9.1.1" class="ltx_text">We choose <math id="S3.SS2.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S3.SS2.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1+rN=\tau+rN(1+\delta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1+rN=\tau+rN(1+\delta)</annotation></semantics></math> or equivalently <math id="S3.SS2.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r=\frac{1-\tau}{N\delta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r=\frac{1-\tau}{N\delta}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then dividing the inflated form by <math id="S3.SS2.p9.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1+rN" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1+rN</annotation></semantics></math>, the singular set will have areas corresponding to ellipsoids of the correct sizes and <math id="S3.SS2.p9.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> will have area <math id="S3.SS2.p9.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>. Furthermore <math id="S3.SS2.p9.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}</annotation></semantics></math> remains symplectic with <math id="S3.SS2.p9.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math>-orthogonal intersections by our assumption on its intersection with <math id="S3.SS2.p9.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence, blowing the singular set back down symplectically, as described in section <a href="#S3.SS1" title="3.1 Background results on ellipsoid embeddings ‣ 3 Packing stability of ellipsoids ‣ Packing stability for symplectic 4-manifolds." class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>, gives our directed symplectic packing as required.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p10.1" class="ltx_p">It remains to show that such a curve <math id="S3.SS2.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math> exists. For this we use the following result from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx9" title="" class="ltx_ref">MO13</a>]</cite>, Corollary 1.2.17.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmthmx3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_thm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmthmx3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem</span></span><span id="Thmthmx3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(McDuff-Opshtein)<span id="Thmthmx3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmthmx3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmthmx3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmthmx3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Suppose <math id="Thmthmx3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> closed balls of sizes <math id="Thmthmx3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p^{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>p</mi><mi>j</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p^{j}</annotation></semantics></math> can be embedded in <math id="Thmthmx3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> and let <math id="Thmthmx3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathbb{P}}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathbb{P}}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the corresponding blow-up with exceptional divisors <math id="Thmthmx3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F^{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>F</mi><mi>j</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F^{j}</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="Thmthmx3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}</annotation></semantics></math> be a singular set in <math id="Thmthmx3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathbb{P}}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathbb{P}}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then the class</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex16" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A=N(L-\sum_{j}p^{j}[F^{j}])" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo rspace="0.055em">−</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mi>j</mi></munder><mrow><msup><mi>p</mi><mi>j</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mi>F</mi><mi>j</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A=N(L-\sum_{j}p^{j}[F^{j}])</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmthmx3.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmthmx3.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">has a connected embedded symplectic representative <math id="Thmthmx3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math> which intersects <math id="Thmthmx3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}</annotation></semantics></math> positively and transversally provided <math id="Thmthmx3.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A\cdot S^{j}\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mi>j</mi></msup></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A\cdot S^{j}\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math> for all component curves <math id="Thmthmx3.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S^{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>S</mi><mi>j</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S^{j}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="Thmthmx3.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p11.1" class="ltx_p">Applying this to our class <math id="S3.SS2.p11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>, the intersection of <math id="S3.SS2.p11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> with the <math id="S3.SS2.p11.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{i,j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{i,j}</annotation></semantics></math> is proportional to their area, and the intersection with <math id="S3.SS2.p11.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{C}</annotation></semantics></math> is positive precisely if our area hypotheses are fulfilled. Hence Theorem <a href="#Thmthmx3" title="Theorem (McDuff-Opshtein). ‣ 3.2 Ellipsoid embeddings in good positions relative to a given curve ‣ 3 Packing stability of ellipsoids ‣ Packing stability for symplectic 4-manifolds." class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span></a> produces a curve in our class. The curve has positive self-intersection because this self-intersection is the volume of the blow-up, following section <a href="#S3.SS1" title="3.1 Background results on ellipsoid embeddings ‣ 3 Packing stability of ellipsoids ‣ Packing stability for symplectic 4-manifolds." class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>, of a <math id="S3.SS2.p11.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math> neighborhood of the original disjoint embedded ellipsoids.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p12" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p12.1" class="ltx_p">This completes the proof of Lemma <a href="#S1.Thmdefinition6" title="Lemma 1.6. ‣ Organization of the paper and description of methods. ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Packing stability for symplectic 4-manifolds." class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.6</span></a>.  <math id="S3.SS2.p12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\square" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">□</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\square</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.3 </span>Proof of theorem <a href="#Thmthm2" title="Theorem 2. ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Packing stability for symplectic 4-manifolds." class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a></h3>

<div id="S3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">By approximating a general ellipsoid by a rational ellipsoid, and using the uniqueness up to isotopy of ball packings of an ellipsoid,
see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx6" title="" class="ltx_ref">McD98</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx9" title="" class="ltx_ref">MO13</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx7" title="" class="ltx_ref">McD09</a>]</cite>, we see that to establish the strong packing stability property it suffices to show the following.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmdefinition3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmdefinition3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 3.3</span></span><span id="S3.Thmdefinition3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmdefinition3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmdefinition3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S3.Thmdefinition3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\forall a\in\mathbb{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\forall a\in\mathbb{Q}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.Thmdefinition3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, <math id="S3.Thmdefinition3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E(1,a)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E(1,a)</annotation></semantics></math> has the strong packing stability.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Proof:</span> Assume without loss of generality that <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a&gt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a&gt;1</annotation></semantics></math>, and consider the standard toric embedding <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>φ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E(a-1,a)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E(a-1,a)</annotation></semantics></math> into <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}^{2}(a)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}^{2}(a)</annotation></semantics></math>, whose image has moment polytope drawn in Figure <a href="#S3.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 3.3 Proof of theorem ‣ 3 Packing stability of ellipsoids ‣ Packing stability for symplectic 4-manifolds." class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>. Here <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}^{2}(a)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}^{2}(a)</annotation></semantics></math> denotes complex projective space with lines scaled to have area <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math>. Notice that the complement of the closure of this ellipsoid is exactly <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E(1,a)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E(1,a)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F1" class="ltx_figure"><span id="S3.F1.pic1" class="ltx_picture ltx_centering" style="width:0.0pt;height:0.0pt;"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" version="1.1" width="0.00" height="0.00" overflow="visible"><g transform="translate(0,0.00) scale(1,-1)"></g></svg></span><span id="S3.F1.pic2" class="ltx_picture ltx_centering" style="width:97.64pt;height:94.08pt;"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" version="1.1" width="129.70" height="124.97" overflow="visible"><g transform="translate(0,124.97) scale(1,-1)"><g transform="translate(-102.84,162.11)"><g transform="translate(0,1.33) scale(1,-1)"><foreignObject width="1.33" height="1.33" overflow="visible"><Math mode="inline" tex="a" text="a" xml:id="S3.F1.pic2.m1">
                  <XMath xml:id="S3.F1.pic2.m1.1">
                    <XMTok font="italic" role="UNKNOWN">a</XMTok>
                  </XMath>
                </Math></foreignObject></g></g><g transform="translate(197.7,-159.13)"><g transform="translate(0,9.17) scale(1,-1)"><foreignObject width="35.07" height="9.17" overflow="visible"><Math mode="inline" tex="a-1" text="a - 1" xml:id="S3.F1.pic2.m2">
                  <XMath xml:id="S3.F1.pic2.m2.1">
                    <XMApp xml:id="S3.F1.pic2.m2.1.1">
                      <XMTok meaning="minus" role="ADDOP">-</XMTok>
                      <XMTok font="italic" role="UNKNOWN">a</XMTok>
                      <XMTok meaning="1" role="NUMBER">1</XMTok>
                    </XMApp>
                  </XMath>
                </Math></foreignObject></g></g><g transform="translate(146.34,-159.13)"><g transform="translate(0,9.17) scale(1,-1)"><foreignObject width="17.67" height="9.17" overflow="visible"><Math mode="inline" tex="1" text="1" xml:id="S3.F1.pic2.m3">
                  <XMath xml:id="S3.F1.pic2.m3.1">
                    <XMTok meaning="1" role="NUMBER">1</XMTok>
                  </XMath>
                </Math></foreignObject></g></g><g transform="translate(174.01,-95.86)"><g transform="translate(0,13.28) scale(1,-1)"><foreignObject width="68.94" height="9.96" overflow="visible"><Math mode="inline" tex="E(a-1,a)" text="E@(vector@(a - 1, a))" xml:id="S3.F1.pic2.m4">
                  <XMath xml:id="S3.F1.pic2.m4.3">
                    <XMApp xml:id="S3.F1.pic2.m4.3.1">
                      <XMTok font="italic" role="UNKNOWN">E</XMTok>
                      <XMDual xml:id="S3.F1.pic2.m4.3.1.2">
                        <XMApp xml:id="S3.F1.pic2.m4.3.1.2.1">
                          <XMTok meaning="vector"/>
                          <XMRef idref="S3.F1.pic2.m4.1" xml:id="S3.F1.pic2.m4.3.1.2.1.2"/>
                          <XMRef idref="S3.F1.pic2.m4.2" xml:id="S3.F1.pic2.m4.3.1.2.1.3"/>
                        </XMApp>
                        <XMWrap xml:id="S3.F1.pic2.m4.3.1.2.2">
                          <XMTok role="OPEN" stretchy="false">(</XMTok>
                          <XMApp xml:id="S3.F1.pic2.m4.1">
                            <XMTok meaning="minus" role="ADDOP">-</XMTok>
                            <XMTok font="italic" role="UNKNOWN">a</XMTok>
                            <XMTok meaning="1" role="NUMBER">1</XMTok>
                          </XMApp>
                          <XMTok role="PUNCT">,</XMTok>
                          <XMTok font="italic" role="UNKNOWN" xml:id="S3.F1.pic2.m4.2">a</XMTok>
                          <XMTok role="CLOSE" stretchy="false">)</XMTok>
                        </XMWrap>
                      </XMDual>
                    </XMApp>
                  </XMath>
                </Math></foreignObject></g></g></g></svg></span>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 1: </span><math id="S3.F1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> as union of two ellipsoids.</figcaption>
</figure>
<section id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Step I: we produce a packing of <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}^{2}(a)\backslash\tau E_{0}(a-1,a)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo rspace="0.222em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">\</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}^{2}(a)\backslash\tau E_{0}(a-1,a)</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau&lt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau&lt;1</annotation></semantics></math>.</h4>

<div id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Consider the sequence <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(w_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(w_{i})</annotation></semantics></math> associated by Theorem <a href="#Thmthm4" title="Theorem 4 (McDuff, []). ‣ 3.1 Background results on ellipsoid embeddings ‣ 3 Packing stability of ellipsoids ‣ Packing stability for symplectic 4-manifolds." class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>
to the ellipsoid <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E(a-1,a)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E(a-1,a)</annotation></semantics></math>. Since the closure of <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E(a-1,a)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E(a-1,a)</annotation></semantics></math> embeds into <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}^{2}(a)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}^{2}(a)</annotation></semantics></math>, so does
<math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sqcup B^{4}(w_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0em">⊔</mo><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sqcup B^{4}(w_{i})</annotation></semantics></math>. Consider the symplectic form <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\omega}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>ω</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\omega}</annotation></semantics></math> obtained by blowing-up these balls. By Lemma <a href="#S1.Thmdefinition5" title="Lemma 1.5. ‣ Organization of the paper and description of methods. ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Packing stability for symplectic 4-manifolds." class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.5</span></a>, <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathbb{P}^{2},\hat{\omega})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>ω</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathbb{P}^{2},\hat{\omega})</annotation></semantics></math> has the packing stability. Thus, for numbers <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{i}&lt;\Lambda(\hat{\omega})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>ω</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{i}&lt;\Lambda(\hat{\omega})</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum\frac{a_{i}^{2}}{2}&lt;\text{Vol}\,(\hat{\mathbb{P}}^{2}_{p},\hat{\omega})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>∑</mo><mfrac><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mtext>Vol</mtext><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>p</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>ω</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum\frac{a_{i}^{2}}{2}&lt;\text{Vol}\,(\hat{\mathbb{P}}^{2}_{p},\hat{\omega})</annotation></semantics></math>, we have <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sqcup B(a_{i})\overset{\omega}{\hookrightarrow}(\hat{\mathbb{P}}^{2},\hat{\omega})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0em">⊔</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">𝜔</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>ω</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sqcup B(a_{i})\overset{\omega}{\hookrightarrow}(\hat{\mathbb{P}}^{2},\hat{\omega})</annotation></semantics></math>.
It is clear (either by Lemma <a href="#S1.Thmdefinition6" title="Lemma 1.6. ‣ Organization of the paper and description of methods. ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Packing stability for symplectic 4-manifolds." class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.6</span></a> or by direct analysis in this case), that these balls also embed into the complement of the exceptional divisors in <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathbb{P}}^{2}_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>p</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathbb{P}}^{2}_{p}</annotation></semantics></math>. Blowing down the exceptional divisors, we then get a packing of <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}^{2}(a)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}^{2}(a)</annotation></semantics></math> by <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sqcup B(w_{i})\sqcup B(a_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0em">⊔</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>⊔</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sqcup B(w_{i})\sqcup B(a_{i})</annotation></semantics></math>, hence by Theorem <a href="#Thmthm4" title="Theorem 4 (McDuff, []). ‣ 3.1 Background results on ellipsoid embeddings ‣ 3 Packing stability of ellipsoids ‣ Packing stability for symplectic 4-manifolds." class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> a packing of <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}^{2}(a)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}^{2}(a)</annotation></semantics></math> by <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E(a-1,a)\sqcup B(a_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊔</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E(a-1,a)\sqcup B(a_{i})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Fix now <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau&lt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau&lt;1</annotation></semantics></math>, consider the induced packing of <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}^{2}(a)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}^{2}(a)</annotation></semantics></math> by <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau E(a-1,a)\sqcup B(a_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊔</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau E(a-1,a)\sqcup B(a_{i})</annotation></semantics></math>, and call <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{1},L_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{1},L_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> the symplectic lines in <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> which correspond to the lines <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p2.m6" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\{w=0\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>w</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{w=0\}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p2.m7" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\{\pi(|z|^{2}+|w|^{2})=a\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>π</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>z</mi><msup><mo fence="false" stretchy="false">|</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">+</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>w</mi><msup><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\pi(|z|^{2}+|w|^{2})=a\}</annotation></semantics></math> on the toric model. Since <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau a</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau(a-1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau(a-1)</annotation></semantics></math> are less than <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math>, Lemma <a href="#S1.Thmdefinition6" title="Lemma 1.6. ‣ Organization of the paper and description of methods. ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Packing stability for symplectic 4-manifolds." class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.6</span></a> ensures that there exists a symplectic embedding <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau E(a-1,a)\sqcup B(a_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊔</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau E(a-1,a)\sqcup B(a_{i})</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> intersects <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau E(a-1,a)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau E(a-1,a)</annotation></semantics></math> along its big axis (of size <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau a</annotation></semantics></math>) and avoids all the balls, while <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> intersects <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p2.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau E(a-1,a)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau E(a-1,a)</annotation></semantics></math> along its small axis and avoids the balls. Arguing as in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ops13a</a>]</cite>, Corollary <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p2.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2.2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2.2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2.2</annotation></semantics></math>, we can isotope <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p2.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi(\tau E(a-1,a))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi(\tau E(a-1,a))</annotation></semantics></math> to the standard embedding <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p2.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>φ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>, keeping <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p2.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{1},L_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{1},L_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> fixed. As a result, we therefore get a packing of <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p2.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi:\sqcup B(a_{i})\overset{\omega}{\hookrightarrow}X:=\mathbb{P}^{2}(a)\backslash(L_{1}\cup L_{2}\cup\varphi_{0}(\tau E(a-1,a)))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mo lspace="0.111em" rspace="0em">⊔</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">𝜔</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo rspace="0.222em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">\</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>∪</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>∪</mo><mrow><msub><mi>φ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi:\sqcup B(a_{i})\overset{\omega}{\hookrightarrow}X:=\mathbb{P}^{2}(a)\backslash(L_{1}\cup L_{2}\cup\varphi_{0}(\tau E(a-1,a)))</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Step II: we get a packing of <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E(1,a)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E(1,a)</annotation></semantics></math>.</h4>

<div id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Consider now the contracting Liouville vector field <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}^{2}\backslash L_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">\</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}^{2}\backslash L_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> which corresponds to the radial vector field <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="-\sum r_{i}\frac{\partial}{\partial r_{i}}=-\sum R_{i}\frac{\partial}{\partial R_{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">−</mo><mo>∑</mo><mi>r</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><mfrac><mo>∂</mo><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.055em">−</mo><mo>∑</mo><mi>R</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><mfrac><mo>∂</mo><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-\sum r_{i}\frac{\partial}{\partial r_{i}}=-\sum R_{i}\frac{\partial}{\partial R_{i}}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B^{4}(a)=\mathbb{P}^{2}(a)\backslash L_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo rspace="0.222em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">\</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B^{4}(a)=\mathbb{P}^{2}(a)\backslash L_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>. A straightforward computation shows that the flow <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi^{f(\tau)}_{X_{0}}\circ\psi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>ψ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi^{f(\tau)}_{X_{0}}\circ\psi</annotation></semantics></math> provides embeddings of symplectic balls of size <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[1-f(\tau)]a_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[1-f(\tau)]a_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> into <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E(1,a)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E(1,a)</annotation></semantics></math>, for some <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(\tau)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(\tau)</annotation></semantics></math> which tends to <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math> as <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau\to 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau\to 1</annotation></semantics></math>. We therefore get packings of <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E(1,a)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E(1,a)</annotation></semantics></math> by balls of size arbitrarily close to <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">As ball packings are unique up to isotopy, see again <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx9" title="" class="ltx_ref">MO13</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx7" title="" class="ltx_ref">McD09</a>]</cite>, a limiting argument implies that we can actually get a packing of the whole open balls.  <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\square" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">□</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\square</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmdefinition4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_rk">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmdefinition4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 3.4</span></span><span id="S3.Thmdefinition4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmdefinition4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmdefinition4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmdefinition4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The previous proof can be roughly described as follows: an ellipsoid is the complement of an ellipsoid in <math id="S3.Thmdefinition4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, the latter being obtained from some specific ball packing in <math id="S3.Thmdefinition4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus packing the first ellipsoid amounts to packing <math id="S3.Thmdefinition4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> with more balls. This argument is completely similar to McDuff’s proof of Hofer’s conjecture <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx8" title="" class="ltx_ref">McD11</a>]</cite>. The difference between the two approaches lies in the justification of this ”thus” in the second sentence of the remark. McDuff refers to her inner/outer approximation technique in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx7" title="" class="ltx_ref">McD09</a>]</cite>, while we base our proof on lemma <a href="#S1.Thmdefinition6" title="Lemma 1.6. ‣ Organization of the paper and description of methods. ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Packing stability for symplectic 4-manifolds." class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.6</span></a>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmdefinition5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmdefinition5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 3.5</span></span><span id="S3.Thmdefinition5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmdefinition5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmdefinition5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmdefinition5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The function <math id="S3.Thmdefinition5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda(a):=\Lambda(E(1,a))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda(a):=\Lambda(E(1,a))</annotation></semantics></math> is locally bounded.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Proof: </span> We need to rework slightly the previous proof. Since we can approximate any ellipsoid by rational ellipsoids from the inside, we need only prove the statement for <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a\in\mathbb{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a\in\mathbb{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>. So let us fix <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a,a^{\prime}\in\mathbb{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>a</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a,a^{\prime}\in\mathbb{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>, and consider the sequences <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(w_{1},\dots,w_{p})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(w_{1},\dots,w_{p})</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(w^{\prime}_{1},\dots,w_{p^{\prime}}^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>w</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>w</mi><msup><mi>p</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(w^{\prime}_{1},\dots,w_{p^{\prime}}^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> associated to the ellipsoids <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E(a-1,a)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E(a-1,a)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E(a^{\prime}-1,a^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>a</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>a</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E(a^{\prime}-1,a^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> by Theorem <a href="#Thmthm4" title="Theorem 4 (McDuff, []). ‣ 3.1 Background results on ellipsoid embeddings ‣ 3 Packing stability of ellipsoids ‣ Packing stability for symplectic 4-manifolds." class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>. By Lemma <a href="#S3.Thmdefinition1" title="Lemma 3.1. ‣ 3.1 Background results on ellipsoid embeddings ‣ 3 Packing stability of ellipsoids ‣ Packing stability for symplectic 4-manifolds." class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>, if we fix <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math> and consider <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>a</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> sufficiently close to <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p^{\prime}&gt;p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>p</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p^{\prime}&gt;p</annotation></semantics></math> and we can ensure that the <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{i}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{i}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> are arbitrarily close to the <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\leq p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\leq p</annotation></semantics></math> and to <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p3.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i&gt;p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i&gt;p</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\omega}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>ω</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\omega}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> be the symplectic form obtained by blowing-up a packing <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B(w_{1}^{\prime})\sqcup\dots\sqcup B(w_{p}^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>w</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊔</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">⋯</mi><mo>⊔</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>w</mi><mi>p</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B(w_{1}^{\prime})\sqcup\dots\sqcup B(w_{p}^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> (here we indeed mean <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{p}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>w</mi><mi>p</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{p}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> and not <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{p^{\prime}}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>w</mi><msup><mi>p</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{p^{\prime}}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>). Lemma <a href="#S1.Thmdefinition5" title="Lemma 1.5. ‣ Organization of the paper and description of methods. ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Packing stability for symplectic 4-manifolds." class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.5</span></a> ensures that there exists <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda_{\hat{\omega}^{\prime}}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>ω</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda_{\hat{\omega}^{\prime}}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> such that for any collection of numbers <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> which abide by the volume constraint and <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{i}&lt;\Lambda(\hat{\omega}^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>ω</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{i}&lt;\Lambda(\hat{\omega}^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> we have</p>
<table id="S3.Ex17" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sqcup B(a_{i})\hookrightarrow(\hat{\mathbb{P}}^{2}_{p},\hat{\omega}^{\prime})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0em">⊔</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>p</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>ω</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sqcup B(a_{i})\hookrightarrow(\hat{\mathbb{P}}^{2}_{p},\hat{\omega}^{\prime}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p4.2" class="ltx_p">Moreover, <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda(\hat{\omega}^{\prime})&gt;\frac{d_{[\hat{\omega}]}}{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>ω</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mfrac><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>ω</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msub><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda(\hat{\omega}^{\prime})&gt;\frac{d_{[\hat{\omega}]}}{2}</annotation></semantics></math> provided that <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(w_{1}^{\prime},\dots,w_{p}^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>w</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>w</mi><mi>p</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(w_{1}^{\prime},\dots,w_{p}^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> are close enough to <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(w_{1},\dots,w_{p})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(w_{1},\dots,w_{p})</annotation></semantics></math> - this is the case provided <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>a</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> is close enough to <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p4.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math>, which we assume henceforth. We recall from Lemma <a href="#S2.Thmdefinition1" title="Lemma 2.1. ‣ 2 Packing stability of rational surfaces ‣ Packing stability for symplectic 4-manifolds." class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.1</span></a> that the quantity <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p4.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{d_{[\hat{\omega}]}}{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mfrac><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>ω</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msub><mn>2</mn></mfrac><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{d_{[\hat{\omega}]}}{2}</annotation></semantics></math> is bounded away from <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p4.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Assuming <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>a</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> even closer to <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math>, we can also ensure that <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{i}^{\prime}&lt;\frac{d_{[\hat{\omega}]}}{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>&lt;</mo><mfrac><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>ω</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msub><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{i}^{\prime}&lt;\frac{d_{[\hat{\omega}]}}{2}</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i&gt;p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i&gt;p</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus, <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}^{2}(1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}^{2}(1)</annotation></semantics></math> has a packing by
<math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sqcup B(w_{i}^{\prime})\sqcup B(a_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0em">⊔</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>⊔</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sqcup B(w_{i}^{\prime})\sqcup B(a_{i})</annotation></semantics></math>,
for any collection of <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(a_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(a_{i})</annotation></semantics></math> such that the volume constraint is satisfied and <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{i}&lt;\frac{d_{[\hat{\omega}]}}{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><mfrac><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>ω</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msub><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{i}&lt;\frac{d_{[\hat{\omega}]}}{2}</annotation></semantics></math>. Arguing as in the previous proof, we get a packing of <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E(1,a^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>a</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E(1,a^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> (<math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\simeq\mathbb{P}^{2}(1)\backslash E(a^{\prime}-1,a^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≃</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo rspace="0.222em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">\</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>a</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>a</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\simeq\mathbb{P}^{2}(1)\backslash E(a^{\prime}-1,a^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math>) by the balls of size <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p5.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, as long as the <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p5.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> are less than <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p5.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{d_{[\hat{\omega}]}}{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mfrac><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>ω</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msub><mn>2</mn></mfrac><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{d_{[\hat{\omega}]}}{2}</annotation></semantics></math> together with the volume constraint. Hence <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p5.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda(a^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>a</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda(a^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> is bounded from below for <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p5.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>a</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> close to <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p5.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math> and the proof is complete.  <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p5.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\square" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">□</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\square</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Packing stability of pseudo-balls <math id="S4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T(a,b,\alpha,\beta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>β</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T(a,b,\alpha,\beta)</annotation></semantics></math></h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We recall the definition of a pseudo-ball as the intersection of two ellipsoids.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmdefinition1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmdefinition1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 4.1</span></span><span id="S4.Thmdefinition1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmdefinition1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmdefinition1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmdefinition1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">A pseudo-ball is a domain <math id="S4.Thmdefinition1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T(a,b,\alpha,\beta)\subset\mathbb{C}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>β</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T(a,b,\alpha,\beta)\subset\mathbb{C}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S4.Thmdefinition1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a&gt;\alpha,b&gt;\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a&gt;\alpha,b&gt;\beta</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.Thmdefinition1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a,b&lt;\alpha+\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a,b&lt;\alpha+\beta</annotation></semantics></math>, defined by</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex18" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{array}[]{l}T(a,b,\alpha,\beta):=\{(z,w)\in\mathbb{C}^{2}\;|\;(\pi|z|^{2},\pi|w|^{2})\in Q(a,b,\alpha,\beta)\},\text{ where}\\
Q(a,b,\alpha,\beta):=\text{Conv}\,\langle(0,0),(0,a),(b,0),(\alpha,\beta)\rangle\subset\mathbb{R}^{2}.\end{array}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>β</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>β</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic"> where</mtext></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>β</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">Conv</mtext><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>β</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{array}[]{l}T(a,b,\alpha,\beta):=\{(z,w)\in\mathbb{C}^{2}\;|\;(\pi|z|^{2},\pi|w|^{2})\in Q(a,b,\alpha,\beta)\},\text{ where}\\
Q(a,b,\alpha,\beta):=\text{Conv}\,\langle(0,0),(0,a),(b,0),(\alpha,\beta)\rangle\subset\mathbb{R}^{2}.\end{array}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Our proof of the packing stability for ellipsoids used the fact that an ellipsoid is always obtained by excising an ellipsoid from <math id="S4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>. We notice that a pseudo-ball is obtained by excising two ellipsoids (see Figure <a href="#S4.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 4 Packing stability of pseudo-balls 𝑇(𝑎,𝑏,𝛼,𝛽) ‣ Packing stability for symplectic 4-manifolds." class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F2" class="ltx_figure"><span id="S4.F2.pic1" class="ltx_picture ltx_centering" style="width:0.0pt;height:0.0pt;"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" version="1.1" width="0.00" height="0.00" overflow="visible"><g transform="translate(0,0.00) scale(1,-1)"></g></svg></span><span id="S4.F2.pic2" class="ltx_picture ltx_centering" style="width:116.46pt;height:117.92999999999999pt;"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" version="1.1" width="154.70" height="156.65" overflow="visible"><g transform="translate(0,156.65) scale(1,-1)"><g transform="translate(-63.55,165.21)"><g transform="translate(0,1.33) scale(1,-1)"><foreignObject width="1.33" height="1.33" overflow="visible"><Math mode="inline" tex="E(\alpha+\beta-a,\beta)" text="E@(vector@((alpha + beta) - a, beta))" xml:id="S4.F2.pic2.m1">
                <XMath xml:id="S4.F2.pic2.m1.3">
                  <XMApp xml:id="S4.F2.pic2.m1.3.1">
                    <XMTok font="italic" role="UNKNOWN">E</XMTok>
                    <XMDual xml:id="S4.F2.pic2.m1.3.1.2">
                      <XMApp xml:id="S4.F2.pic2.m1.3.1.2.1">
                        <XMTok meaning="vector"/>
                        <XMRef idref="S4.F2.pic2.m1.1" xml:id="S4.F2.pic2.m1.3.1.2.1.2"/>
                        <XMRef idref="S4.F2.pic2.m1.2" xml:id="S4.F2.pic2.m1.3.1.2.1.3"/>
                      </XMApp>
                      <XMWrap xml:id="S4.F2.pic2.m1.3.1.2.2">
                        <XMTok role="OPEN" stretchy="false">(</XMTok>
                        <XMApp xml:id="S4.F2.pic2.m1.1">
                          <XMTok meaning="minus" role="ADDOP">-</XMTok>
                          <XMApp xml:id="S4.F2.pic2.m1.1.2">
                            <XMTok meaning="plus" role="ADDOP">+</XMTok>
                            <XMTok font="italic" name="alpha" role="UNKNOWN">α</XMTok>
                            <XMTok font="italic" name="beta" role="UNKNOWN">β</XMTok>
                          </XMApp>
                          <XMTok font="italic" role="UNKNOWN">a</XMTok>
                        </XMApp>
                        <XMTok role="PUNCT">,</XMTok>
                        <XMTok font="italic" name="beta" role="UNKNOWN" xml:id="S4.F2.pic2.m1.2">β</XMTok>
                        <XMTok role="CLOSE" stretchy="false">)</XMTok>
                      </XMWrap>
                    </XMDual>
                  </XMApp>
                </XMath>
              </Math></foreignObject></g></g><g transform="translate(85.97,-47.36)"><g transform="translate(0,13.28) scale(1,-1)"><foreignObject width="94.71" height="9.96" overflow="visible"><Math mode="inline" tex="E(\alpha,\alpha+\beta-b)" text="E@(vector@(alpha, (alpha + beta) - b))" xml:id="S4.F2.pic2.m2">
                <XMath xml:id="S4.F2.pic2.m2.3">
                  <XMApp xml:id="S4.F2.pic2.m2.3.1">
                    <XMTok font="italic" role="UNKNOWN">E</XMTok>
                    <XMDual xml:id="S4.F2.pic2.m2.3.1.2">
                      <XMApp xml:id="S4.F2.pic2.m2.3.1.2.1">
                        <XMTok meaning="vector"/>
                        <XMRef idref="S4.F2.pic2.m2.1" xml:id="S4.F2.pic2.m2.3.1.2.1.2"/>
                        <XMRef idref="S4.F2.pic2.m2.2" xml:id="S4.F2.pic2.m2.3.1.2.1.3"/>
                      </XMApp>
                      <XMWrap xml:id="S4.F2.pic2.m2.3.1.2.2">
                        <XMTok role="OPEN" stretchy="false">(</XMTok>
                        <XMTok font="italic" name="alpha" role="UNKNOWN" xml:id="S4.F2.pic2.m2.1">α</XMTok>
                        <XMTok role="PUNCT">,</XMTok>
                        <XMApp xml:id="S4.F2.pic2.m2.2">
                          <XMTok meaning="minus" role="ADDOP">-</XMTok>
                          <XMApp xml:id="S4.F2.pic2.m2.2.2">
                            <XMTok meaning="plus" role="ADDOP">+</XMTok>
                            <XMTok font="italic" name="alpha" role="UNKNOWN">α</XMTok>
                            <XMTok font="italic" name="beta" role="UNKNOWN">β</XMTok>
                          </XMApp>
                          <XMTok font="italic" role="UNKNOWN">b</XMTok>
                        </XMApp>
                        <XMTok role="CLOSE" stretchy="false">)</XMTok>
                      </XMWrap>
                    </XMDual>
                  </XMApp>
                </XMath>
              </Math></foreignObject></g></g><g transform="translate(143.88,-84.72)"><g transform="translate(0,13.28) scale(1,-1)"><foreignObject width="43.30" height="9.96" overflow="visible"><Math mode="inline" tex="(\alpha,\beta)" text="open-interval@(alpha, beta)" xml:id="S4.F2.pic2.m3">
                <XMath xml:id="S4.F2.pic2.m3.3">
                  <XMDual xml:id="S4.F2.pic2.m3.3.1">
                    <XMApp xml:id="S4.F2.pic2.m3.3.1.1">
                      <XMTok meaning="open-interval"/>
                      <XMRef idref="S4.F2.pic2.m3.1" xml:id="S4.F2.pic2.m3.3.1.1.2"/>
                      <XMRef idref="S4.F2.pic2.m3.2" xml:id="S4.F2.pic2.m3.3.1.1.3"/>
                    </XMApp>
                    <XMWrap xml:id="S4.F2.pic2.m3.3.1.2">
                      <XMTok role="OPEN" stretchy="false">(</XMTok>
                      <XMTok font="italic" name="alpha" role="UNKNOWN" xml:id="S4.F2.pic2.m3.1">α</XMTok>
                      <XMTok role="PUNCT">,</XMTok>
                      <XMTok font="italic" name="beta" role="UNKNOWN" xml:id="S4.F2.pic2.m3.2">β</XMTok>
                      <XMTok role="CLOSE" stretchy="false">)</XMTok>
                    </XMWrap>
                  </XMDual>
                </XMath>
              </Math></foreignObject></g></g><g transform="translate(173.77,-163.18)"><g transform="translate(0,9.17) scale(1,-1)"><foreignObject width="18.07" height="9.17" overflow="visible"><Math mode="inline" tex="a" text="a" xml:id="S4.F2.pic2.m4">
                <XMath xml:id="S4.F2.pic2.m4.1">
                  <XMTok font="italic" role="UNKNOWN">a</XMTok>
                </XMath>
              </Math></foreignObject></g></g><g transform="translate(63.55,-62.31)"><g transform="translate(0,9.17) scale(1,-1)"><foreignObject width="16.74" height="9.17" overflow="visible"><Math mode="inline" tex="b" text="b" xml:id="S4.F2.pic2.m5">
                <XMath xml:id="S4.F2.pic2.m5.1">
                  <XMTok font="italic" role="UNKNOWN">b</XMTok>
                </XMath>
              </Math></foreignObject></g></g><g transform="translate(82.23,-112.74)"><g transform="translate(0,13.28) scale(1,-1)"><foreignObject width="77.44" height="9.96" overflow="visible"><Math mode="inline" tex="T(a,b,\alpha,\beta)" text="T@(vector@(a, b, alpha, beta))" xml:id="S4.F2.pic2.m6">
                <XMath xml:id="S4.F2.pic2.m6.5">
                  <XMApp xml:id="S4.F2.pic2.m6.5.1">
                    <XMTok font="italic" role="UNKNOWN">T</XMTok>
                    <XMDual xml:id="S4.F2.pic2.m6.5.1.2">
                      <XMApp xml:id="S4.F2.pic2.m6.5.1.2.1">
                        <XMTok meaning="vector"/>
                        <XMRef idref="S4.F2.pic2.m6.1" xml:id="S4.F2.pic2.m6.5.1.2.1.2"/>
                        <XMRef idref="S4.F2.pic2.m6.2" xml:id="S4.F2.pic2.m6.5.1.2.1.3"/>
                        <XMRef idref="S4.F2.pic2.m6.3" xml:id="S4.F2.pic2.m6.5.1.2.1.4"/>
                        <XMRef idref="S4.F2.pic2.m6.4" xml:id="S4.F2.pic2.m6.5.1.2.1.5"/>
                      </XMApp>
                      <XMWrap xml:id="S4.F2.pic2.m6.5.1.2.2">
                        <XMTok role="OPEN" stretchy="false">(</XMTok>
                        <XMTok font="italic" role="UNKNOWN" xml:id="S4.F2.pic2.m6.1">a</XMTok>
                        <XMTok role="PUNCT">,</XMTok>
                        <XMTok font="italic" role="UNKNOWN" xml:id="S4.F2.pic2.m6.2">b</XMTok>
                        <XMTok role="PUNCT">,</XMTok>
                        <XMTok font="italic" name="alpha" role="UNKNOWN" xml:id="S4.F2.pic2.m6.3">α</XMTok>
                        <XMTok role="PUNCT">,</XMTok>
                        <XMTok font="italic" name="beta" role="UNKNOWN" xml:id="S4.F2.pic2.m6.4">β</XMTok>
                        <XMTok role="CLOSE" stretchy="false">)</XMTok>
                      </XMWrap>
                    </XMDual>
                  </XMApp>
                </XMath>
              </Math></foreignObject></g></g></g></svg></span>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 2: </span><math id="S4.F2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> as union of two ellipsoids and the pseudo-ball.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">To be precise we have the following.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmdefinition2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmdefinition2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 4.2</span></span><span id="S4.Thmdefinition2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmdefinition2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmdefinition2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S4.Thmdefinition2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T(a,b,\alpha,\beta)=\mathbb{P}^{2}(\alpha+\beta)\backslash(E\cup E^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>β</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">\</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>∪</mo><msup><mi>E</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T(a,b,\alpha,\beta)=\mathbb{P}^{2}(\alpha+\beta)\backslash(E\cup E^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.Thmdefinition2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, where <math id="S4.Thmdefinition2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E,E^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>E</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E,E^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> are the toric ellipsoids with equations</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex19" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{array}[]{l}\pi(E^{\prime}):=\text{Conv}\,\langle(0,b),(0,\alpha+\beta),(\alpha,\beta)\rangle,\\
\pi(E):=\text{Conv}\,\langle(a,0),(\alpha+\beta,0),(\alpha,\beta)\rangle.\end{array}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>E</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">Conv</mtext><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>β</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">Conv</mtext><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>β</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{array}[]{l}\pi(E^{\prime}):=\text{Conv}\,\langle(0,b),(0,\alpha+\beta),(\alpha,\beta)\rangle,\\
\pi(E):=\text{Conv}\,\langle(a,0),(\alpha+\beta,0),(\alpha,\beta)\rangle.\end{array}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Moreover, arguing again as in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ops13a</a>]</cite>, we see that these domains are the complement in <math id="S4.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> of <span id="S4.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">any</span> such ellipsoids which are in a good position with the coordinate axis.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmdefinition3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_prop">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmdefinition3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 4.3</span></span><span id="S4.Thmdefinition3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmdefinition3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmdefinition3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmdefinition3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S4.Thmdefinition3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{1},L_{2},L_{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{1},L_{2},L_{3}</annotation></semantics></math> be three lines in <math id="S4.Thmdefinition3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}^{2}(\alpha+\beta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}^{2}(\alpha+\beta)</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S4.Thmdefinition3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>E</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E</annotation></semantics></math> be an embedding of the ellipsoid <math id="S4.Thmdefinition3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E(\alpha+\beta-a,\alpha)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E(\alpha+\beta-a,\alpha)</annotation></semantics></math> into <math id="S4.Thmdefinition3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> whose axis are discs <math id="S4.Thmdefinition3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{1},D_{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{1},D_{3}</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S4.Thmdefinition3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{1},L_{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{1},L_{3}</annotation></semantics></math> of area <math id="S4.Thmdefinition3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha+\beta-a,\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha+\beta-a,\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> respectively. Let <math id="S4.Thmdefinition3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>E</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> be an embedding of the ellipsoid <math id="S4.Thmdefinition3.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E(\alpha+\beta-b,\beta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>β</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E(\alpha+\beta-b,\beta)</annotation></semantics></math> whose axis are discs <math id="S4.Thmdefinition3.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{2}^{\prime},D^{\prime}_{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>D</mi><mn>2</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>D</mi><mn>3</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{2}^{\prime},D^{\prime}_{3}</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S4.Thmdefinition3.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{2},L_{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{2},L_{3}</annotation></semantics></math> of area <math id="S4.Thmdefinition3.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha+\beta-b,\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha+\beta-b,\beta</annotation></semantics></math> respectively (notice that the discs <math id="S4.Thmdefinition3.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{3},D_{3}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>D</mi><mn>3</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{3},D_{3}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> must cover <math id="S4.Thmdefinition3.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{3}</annotation></semantics></math>). Then, <math id="S4.Thmdefinition3.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}^{2}(\alpha+\beta)\backslash(E\cup E^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">\</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>∪</mo><msup><mi>E</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}^{2}(\alpha+\beta)\backslash(E\cup E^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> is symplectomorphic to <math id="S4.Thmdefinition3.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T(a,b,\alpha,\beta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>β</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T(a,b,\alpha,\beta)</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p5.1" class="ltx_p">With this in hand, the proof of Theorem <a href="#Thmthm3" title="Theorem 3. ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Packing stability for symplectic 4-manifolds." class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> is completely analogous to the packing stability of the ellipsoids, and so we do not repeat it here.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Packing stability of irrational closed symplectic <math id="S5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math>-manifolds</h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The object of this section is the proof of Theorem <a href="#Thmthm1" title="Theorem 1. ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Packing stability for symplectic 4-manifolds." class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>. We begin with the general decomposition of symplectic <math id="S5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math>-manifolds, then derive packing stability as a consequence of this and our results for open manifolds.</p>
</div>
<section id="S5.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.1 </span>Decomposition of symplectic <math id="S5.SS1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math>-manifolds with irrational symplectic class</h3>

<div id="S5.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We have already referred to the fact that a rational symplectic manifold is always fully packed by a single ellipsoid. In particular, strong packing stability for ellipsoids implies strong packing stability for all rational symplectic manifolds. Since Conjecture <a href="#Thmconj1" title="Conjecture 1. ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Packing stability for symplectic 4-manifolds." class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> remains open packing stability for ellipsoids does not necessarily imply the same for irrational manifolds, but we now recall some results obtained in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ops13b</a>]</cite> which allow us to split a symplectic manifold with irrational symplectic class into finitely many standard pieces, namely ellipsoids and pseudo-balls. As before, the packing stability property for an irrational symplectic manifold will follow from
the packing properties of these standard pieces.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmdefinition1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmdefinition1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 5.1</span></span><span id="S5.Thmdefinition1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmdefinition1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmdefinition1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.Thmdefinition1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">A singular polarization of a symplectic <math id="S5.Thmdefinition1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math>-manifold <math id="S5.Thmdefinition1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> is a weighted multi-curve <math id="S5.Thmdefinition1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf\Sigma}=(\Sigma_{i},\alpha_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝚺</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf\Sigma}=(\Sigma_{i},\alpha_{i})</annotation></semantics></math>, where:</span></p>
<ul id="S5.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S5.I1.ix1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><math id="S5.I1.ix1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bullet" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo mathsize="0.500em">∙</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bullet</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.I1.ix1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:50%;"> </span></span> 
<div id="S5.I1.ix1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I1.ix1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S5.I1.ix1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega:=[\omega]=\sum\alpha_{i}\textnormal{\small PD}([\Sigma_{i}])" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><mo>∑</mo><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext mathsize="0.900em">PD</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega:=[\omega]=\sum\alpha_{i}\textnormal{\small PD}([\Sigma_{i}])</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.I1.ix1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">,</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I1.ix2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><math id="S5.I1.ix2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bullet" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo mathsize="0.500em">∙</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bullet</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.I1.ix2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:50%;"> </span></span> 
<div id="S5.I1.ix2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I1.ix2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.I1.ix2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Each </span><math id="S5.I1.ix2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma_{i}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.I1.ix2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is an embedded symplectic curve,</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I1.ix3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><math id="S5.I1.ix3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bullet" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo mathsize="0.500em">∙</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bullet</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.I1.ix3.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:50%;"> </span></span> 
<div id="S5.I1.ix3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I1.ix3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S5.I1.ix3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma_{i}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.I1.ix3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> intersects </span><math id="S5.I1.ix3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma_{j}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.I1.ix3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> transversally and positively,</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I1.ix4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><math id="S5.I1.ix4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bullet" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo mathsize="0.500em">∙</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bullet</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.I1.ix4.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:50%;"> </span></span> 
<div id="S5.I1.ix4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I1.ix4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S5.I1.ix4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma_{i}\cap\Sigma_{j}\cap\Sigma_{k}=\emptyset" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∩</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>∩</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma_{i}\cap\Sigma_{j}\cap\Sigma_{k}=\emptyset</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.I1.ix4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmthmx4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_thm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmthmx4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem</span></span><span id="Thmthmx4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ops13b</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ops11</a>]</cite>)<span id="Thmthmx4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmthmx4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmthmx4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmthmx4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Any closed symplectic <math id="Thmthmx4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math>-manifold <math id="Thmthmx4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> has a singular polarization <math id="Thmthmx4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\bf\Sigma,\alpha})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝚺</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\bf\Sigma,\alpha})</annotation></semantics></math>. The complement of <math id="Thmthmx4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\cup\Sigma_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0em">∪</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\cup\Sigma_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="Thmthmx4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> is endowed with a tame Liouville form <math id="Thmthmx4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> with residues <math id="Thmthmx4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> at <math id="Thmthmx4.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The precise definition of the residues is not so important here, and can be found in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ops11</a>]</cite>. The vector field dual to <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> is a (contracting) Liouville vector field <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>λ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math>, which points outwards along <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\cup\Sigma_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0em">∪</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\cup\Sigma_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>. It is therefore forward complete. The basin of repulsion of a subset <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\subset\cup\Sigma_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo rspace="0.1389em">⊂</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0em">∪</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\subset\cup\Sigma_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is defined as</p>
<table id="S5.Ex20" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex20.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathbb{\mathcal{B}}_{\lambda}(X):=\{p\in M\;|\;\exists t_{0}&lt;0,\;\Phi_{X_{\lambda}}^{t}(p)\underset{t\to t_{0}}{\longrightarrow}X\}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mi>λ</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>M</mi><mo fence="false" lspace="0.280em" rspace="0.447em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">∃</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo rspace="0.447em">,</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>λ</mi></msub><mi>t</mi></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mo stretchy="false">⟶</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em">t</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em" stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">t</mi><mn mathsize="0.710em">0</mn></msub></mrow></munder><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{\mathcal{B}}_{\lambda}(X):=\{p\in M\;|\;\exists t_{0}&lt;0,\;\Phi_{X_{\lambda}}^{t}(p)\underset{t\to t_{0}}{\longrightarrow}X\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">As we see next proposition, the basins of repulsion of well-chosen subsets fully pack <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>, and are standard.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmdefinition2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_prop">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmdefinition2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 5.2</span></span><span id="S5.Thmdefinition2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmdefinition2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmdefinition2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.Thmdefinition2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The basin of attraction of a disc <math id="S5.Thmdefinition2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{D}(a_{i})\subset\Sigma_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔻</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{D}(a_{i})\subset\Sigma_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is an embedded ellipsoid <math id="S5.Thmdefinition2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E(a_{i},\alpha_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E(a_{i},\alpha_{i})</annotation></semantics></math>. The basin of attraction of a cross <math id="S5.Thmdefinition2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{D}(a_{i})\cup\mathbb{D}(a_{j})\subset\Sigma_{i}\cup\Sigma_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔻</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mi>𝔻</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∪</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{D}(a_{i})\cup\mathbb{D}(a_{j})\subset\Sigma_{i}\cup\Sigma_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> (the two discs intersect at exactly one point) is an embedded pseudo-ball <math id="S5.Thmdefinition2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T(a_{i},a_{j},\alpha_{j},\alpha_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T(a_{i},a_{j},\alpha_{j},\alpha_{i})</annotation></semantics></math>. Finally, if a family of discs and crosses cover the polarization up to area <math id="S5.Thmdefinition2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math>, their basin of attraction cover <math id="S5.Thmdefinition2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> up to volume <math id="S5.Thmdefinition2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Proof:</span> Since the flow <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi^{t}_{X_{\lambda}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>λ</mi></msub><mi>t</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi^{t}_{X_{\lambda}}</annotation></semantics></math> is forward complete and contracts the symplectic form, it is obvious that the complement of the basin of repulsion of the whole polarization has zero volume. On the other hand, the basin of repulsion of a subset of the polarization with zero-area has zero volume. We conclude that if discs and crosses cover the polarization up to area <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math>, their basins of repulsion cover <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> up to volume <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math> (we refer to <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ops11</a>]</cite> for more details).</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">It is moreover explained in this paper that these basins of repulsion can be computed in <span id="S5.SS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">any</span> polarization, of any manifold, provided that these polarizations have the same weights. In particular, the basin of repulsion of <math id="S5.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{D}(a_{i})\subset\Sigma_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔻</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{D}(a_{i})\subset\Sigma_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is symplectomorphic to the basin of repulsion of <math id="S5.SS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{D}(a_{i})\times\{0\}\subset\mathbb{C}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔻</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{D}(a_{i})\times\{0\}\subset\mathbb{C}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, with Liouville form <math id="S5.SS1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{i}d\theta_{2}-r_{1}^{2}d\theta_{1}-r_{2}^{2}d\theta_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{i}d\theta_{2}-r_{1}^{2}d\theta_{1}-r_{2}^{2}d\theta_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>. (Note that the Liouville form has residue <math id="S5.SS1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> about the <math id="S5.SS1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> axis.) A computation done in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ops13b</a>]</cite> (Proposition 3.5) shows that this basin of repulsion is the ellipsoid <math id="S5.SS1.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}(a_{i},\alpha_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}(a_{i},\alpha_{i})</annotation></semantics></math>. The basin of repulsion of a cross <math id="S5.SS1.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{D}(a_{i})\times\mathbb{D}(a_{j})\subset\Sigma_{i}\times\Sigma_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔻</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mi>𝔻</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{D}(a_{i})\times\mathbb{D}(a_{j})\subset\Sigma_{i}\times\Sigma_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> is symplectomorphic to the basin of repulsion of <math id="S5.SS1.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{D}(a_{i})\times\{0\}\cup\{0\}\times\mathbb{D}(a_{j})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔻</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mi>𝔻</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{D}(a_{i})\times\{0\}\cup\{0\}\times\mathbb{D}(a_{j})</annotation></semantics></math>, for the Liouville form <math id="S5.SS1.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{i}d\theta_{2}+\alpha_{j}d\theta_{1}-R_{1}d\theta_{1}-R_{2}d\theta_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{i}d\theta_{2}+\alpha_{j}d\theta_{1}-R_{1}d\theta_{1}-R_{2}d\theta_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S5.SS1.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{i}=r_{i}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{i}=r_{i}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>. (The Liouville form now has residues <math id="S5.SS1.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> about the <math id="S5.SS1.p4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> axis and <math id="S5.SS1.p4.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> about the <math id="S5.SS1.p4.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> axis.) The dual vector field is</p>
<table id="S5.Ex21" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex21.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{\lambda}=(\alpha_{j}-R_{1})\frac{\partial}{\partial R_{1}}+(\alpha_{i}-R_{2})\frac{\partial}{\partial R_{2}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>λ</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mfrac><mo>∂</mo><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mfrac><mo>∂</mo><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{\lambda}=(\alpha_{j}-R_{1})\frac{\partial}{\partial R_{1}}+(\alpha_{i}-R_{2})\frac{\partial}{\partial R_{2}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS1.p4.2" class="ltx_p">Therefore, the trajectories are</p>
<table id="S5.Ex22" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex22.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi^{t}_{X_{\lambda}}(R_{1},\theta_{1},R_{2},\theta_{2})=(\alpha_{j}+(R_{1}-\alpha_{j})e^{-t},\theta_{1},\alpha_{i}+(R_{2}-\alpha_{i})e^{-t},\theta_{2})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>λ</mi></msub><mi>t</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi^{t}_{X_{\lambda}}(R_{1},\theta_{1},R_{2},\theta_{2})=(\alpha_{j}+(R_{1}-\alpha_{j})e^{-t},\theta_{1},\alpha_{i}+(R_{2}-\alpha_{i})e^{-t},\theta_{2}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS1.p4.3" class="ltx_p">Thus, this vector field commutes with the classical Hamiltonian toric action on <math id="S5.SS1.p4.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{C}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{C}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, and
in toric coordinates, the trajectories are straight segments whose common endpoint as <math id="S5.SS1.p4.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> goes to <math id="S5.SS1.p4.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+\infty</annotation></semantics></math> is the point <math id="S5.SS1.p4.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\alpha_{j},\alpha_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\alpha_{j},\alpha_{i})</annotation></semantics></math>. Since the cross is the preimage by the toric projection of <math id="S5.SS1.p4.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[0,a_{i}]\times\{0\}\cup\{0\}\times[0,a_{j}]\subset\mathbb{R}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[0,a_{i}]\times\{0\}\cup\{0\}\times[0,a_{j}]\subset\mathbb{R}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, its basin of repulsion is the preimage of the convex hull of <math id="S5.SS1.p4.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{(0,0),(a_{i},0),(0,a_{j}),(\alpha_{j},\alpha_{i})\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{(0,0),(a_{i},0),(0,a_{j}),(\alpha_{j},\alpha_{i})\}</annotation></semantics></math>, by the toric projection, and hence is indeed <math id="S5.SS1.p4.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T(a_{i},a_{j},\alpha_{j},\alpha_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T(a_{i},a_{j},\alpha_{j},\alpha_{i})</annotation></semantics></math>. <math id="S5.SS1.p4.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\square" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">□</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\square</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.2 </span>Proof of theorem <a href="#Thmthm1" title="Theorem 1. ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Packing stability for symplectic 4-manifolds." class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a></h3>

<div id="S5.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> a closed symplectic manifold, on which we lose no generality by assuming its volume to be <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Fix a singular polarization <math id="S5.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf\Sigma}=\{(\Sigma_{i},\alpha_{i}),i=1\dots l\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝚺</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf\Sigma}=\{(\Sigma_{i},\alpha_{i}),i=1\dots l\}</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S5.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}_{\omega}(\Sigma_{i})\geq 10\alpha_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>ω</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mn>10</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}_{\omega}(\Sigma_{i})\geq 10\alpha_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> <math id="S5.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\forall i,j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\forall i,j</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S5.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma_{i}\cap\Sigma_{j}\neq\emptyset" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∩</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma_{i}\cap\Sigma_{j}\neq\emptyset</annotation></semantics></math> <math id="S5.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\forall(i,j)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>∀</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\forall(i,j)</annotation></semantics></math>. In fact, the construction proposed in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ops13b</a>]</cite> provides singular polarizations that automatically satisfy these two constraints: they intersect a lot, the area of the curves are very large, and the <math id="S5.SS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> are very small. Now consider points <math id="S5.SS2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{i}\in\Sigma_{i}\cap\Sigma_{i+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∩</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{i}\in\Sigma_{i}\cap\Sigma_{i+1}</annotation></semantics></math> (with <math id="S5.SS2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma_{l+1}=\Sigma_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma_{l+1}=\Sigma_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>), and discs <math id="S5.SS2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{i},D_{i,i-1},D_{i,i+1}\subset\Sigma_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>⊂</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{i},D_{i,i-1},D_{i,i+1}\subset\Sigma_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> with areas <math id="S5.SS2.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}_{i},\mathcal{A}_{i,i-1},\mathcal{A}_{i,i+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}_{i},\mathcal{A}_{i,i-1},\mathcal{A}_{i,i+1}</annotation></semantics></math> respectively, such that:</p>
<ol id="S5.I2" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S5.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="S5.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The discs are disjoint, and their union has full area in <math id="S5.I2.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> <math id="S5.I2.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\forall i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\forall i</annotation></semantics></math>,</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="S5.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S5.I2.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{i-1}\in D_{i,i-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{i-1}\in D_{i,i-1}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.I2.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{i}\in D_{i,i+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{i}\in D_{i,i+1}</annotation></semantics></math>,</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I2.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">3.</span> 
<div id="S5.I2.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I2.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S5.I2.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{A}_{i,i\pm 1}\in]\alpha_{i\pm 1},\alpha_{i}+\alpha_{i\pm 1}[" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>±</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0em">∈</mo><mo stretchy="false">]</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>±</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>±</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">[</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}_{i,i\pm 1}\in]\alpha_{i\pm 1},\alpha_{i}+\alpha_{i\pm 1}[</annotation></semantics></math>.
Thus
the domains
<math id="S5.I2.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T(\mathcal{A}_{i,i+1},\mathcal{A}_{i+1,i},\alpha_{i+1},\alpha_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T(\mathcal{A}_{i,i+1},\mathcal{A}_{i+1,i},\alpha_{i+1},\alpha_{i})</annotation></semantics></math> are pseudo-balls (see figure <a href="#S5.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 5.2 Proof of theorem ‣ 5 Packing stability of irrational closed symplectic 4-manifolds ‣ Packing stability for symplectic 4-manifolds." class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>).</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
<p id="S5.SS2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">Condition <math id="S5.SS2.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math> is easily achieved by choosing the discs inductively so that these conditions hold (choose first <math id="S5.SS2.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="S5.SS2.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{1,2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{1,2}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.SS2.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.SS2.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{2,3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{2,3}</annotation></semantics></math> …). Note that in order to satisfy condition <math id="S5.SS2.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>, the area <math id="S5.SS2.p2.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}_{1,2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}_{1,2}</annotation></semantics></math> is determined by <math id="S5.SS2.p2.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>; the areas <math id="S5.SS2.p2.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}_{2,1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}_{2,1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS2.p2.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> determine <math id="S5.SS2.p2.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}_{2,3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}_{2,3}</annotation></semantics></math> and so on.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.F3" class="ltx_figure"><span id="S5.F3.pic1" class="ltx_picture ltx_centering" style="width:0.0pt;height:0.0pt;"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" version="1.1" width="0.00" height="0.00" overflow="visible"><g transform="translate(0,0.00) scale(1,-1)"></g></svg></span><span id="S5.F3.pic2" class="ltx_picture ltx_centering" style="width:168.72pt;height:103.28pt;"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" version="1.1" width="224.12" height="137.19" overflow="visible"><g transform="translate(0,137.19) scale(1,-1)"><g transform="translate(-28.14,424.46)"><g transform="translate(0,1.33) scale(1,-1)"><foreignObject width="1.33" height="1.33" overflow="visible"><Math mode="inline" tex="D(2,1)" text="D@(vector@(2, 1))" xml:id="S5.F3.pic2.m1">
